In this class what we will attempt to do is to build a more complex application

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:22.700
to kind of simulate what would it take to bring, let's say, a single-core monolithic application

00:11:22.700 --> 00:11:25.159
and transform it into a distributed application.

00:11:27.659 --> 00:11:30.799
We will try to pay attention to the design goals.

00:11:30.899 --> 00:11:32.740
So what I mentioned in the lecture recording as well,

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:37.159
there are some goals that people follow when they build distributed systems,

00:11:37.820 --> 00:11:39.279
sharing of resources.

00:11:39.500 --> 00:11:42.419
We want to build distributed systems because we want to share resources.

00:11:42.419 --> 00:11:50.360
We have a bunch of computers and we want to be able to combine the computational and data resources that we have available over all of them.

00:11:50.919 --> 00:11:58.959
We want to be able to achieve distribution transparency where basically we have a bunch of computers.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:07.939
If you remember the definition from the first lecture, we said that a distributed system is a collection of independent computers

00:12:07.940 --> 00:12:13.440
that appear to each user one single coherent system, right?

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:17.520
So distribution transparency will refer to that ability

00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:19.900
to hide away the complexity of the system

00:12:19.900 --> 00:12:24.320
and expose to the user a single uniform interface that they can access.

00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:26.520
So you'll have to do the exact same thing.

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:28.160
So you'll provide one single interface,

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:32.020
but underneath the hood, right, your system will change and it will evolve

00:12:32.020 --> 00:12:33.580
and it will become more and more distributed.

00:12:33.580 --> 00:12:38.840
And then of course we will also take a look at size skin ability as well which we haven't

00:12:38.840 --> 00:12:42.980
covered yet so this is one of the design goals that we'll talk about as well.

00:12:42.980 --> 00:12:48.220
And then openness of course right so an important aspect is we want the distributed system or

00:12:48.220 --> 00:12:55.340
the application that you are building, we want it to be easy to maintain, easy to extend

00:12:55.340 --> 00:12:58.640
and easy to interoperate with other applications.

00:12:58.640 --> 00:13:02.820
So throughout the assignments you will start with a very simple program and depending on

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:07.000
how well you've designed the software implementation of your program,

00:13:07.260 --> 00:13:09.379
it will be either easier or harder for you to extend.

00:13:09.580 --> 00:13:12.960
But this would be a good training exercise to learn what it takes

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:14.660
to actually build a more complex application.

