Now, the disadvantage, right, secondary storage is that it's usually way slower than main memory.

00:52:43.819 --> 00:52:46.319
So what we want to do is we want to bring the data.

00:52:46.480 --> 00:52:54.579
If you remember the memory hierarchy from the previous classes, we want to bring data from very slow, high capacity devices closer and closer to the CPU.

00:52:54.980 --> 00:52:56.079
Right. OK, perfect.

00:52:56.079 --> 00:52:58.900
So these are the things that I'd like you to keep in mind.

00:52:59.019 --> 00:53:02.639
And then there's one more type of components that we have in the computer.

00:53:02.759 --> 00:53:04.440
So we have the CPU.

00:53:04.719 --> 00:53:05.380
We have memory.

00:53:06.000 --> 00:53:10.480
And I'm going to add another type of component, which is the motherboard that interconnects everything.

00:53:10.860 --> 00:53:13.880
But then we also have some additional devices, right?

00:53:13.920 --> 00:53:16.039
And we call these input-output devices.

00:53:16.819 --> 00:53:16.960
Right.

00:53:16.960 --> 00:53:22.420
So these are the devices that allow either users or other computers to interact with the computer system.

00:53:22.420 --> 00:53:32.460
So input-output devices could mean keyboards, mice, touchpad, displays, but it can also mean network cards, hard drives, accelerators like GPUs.

00:53:33.260 --> 00:53:42.760
Although the GPUs, they're not really classified as an input-output device, they're more classified as an accelerator, so an extension to the CPU.

00:53:43.340 --> 00:53:51.920
Okay, very good. So this is what I'd like you to have in mind, because in this class, when we learn about distributed systems, and we'll talk today about size scalability,

00:53:52.960 --> 00:54:04.320
We will learn that, again, one of the reasons why we want to build distributed systems is to improve the performance of a service or to be able to provide a service to increasing number of users.

00:54:04.900 --> 00:54:09.920
And a lot of the times, the limitations have to do with these exact components inside our system.

00:54:10.280 --> 00:54:13.840
So sometimes maybe the application that we're building requires a lot of memory.

00:54:14.320 --> 00:54:22.159
So this means that we want to add more computers connected to a network to make a distributed system in order to increase the memory capacity.

00:54:22.420 --> 00:54:25.720
Or sometimes maybe needs more computational power, right?

00:54:25.760 --> 00:54:29.099
So we add more processing power, no GPUs, more CPUs.

00:54:29.300 --> 00:54:30.920
We have examples already, right?

00:54:31.039 --> 00:54:34.240
The memory is needed by AI applications nowadays, right?

00:54:34.240 --> 00:54:36.480
To be able to load models on your system, right?

00:54:36.480 --> 00:54:37.400
You need a lot of RAM.

00:54:37.980 --> 00:54:41.320
You can do that by either buying a very expensive, you know,

00:54:41.360 --> 00:54:43.440
single-node computer with a lot of RAM.

00:54:43.860 --> 00:54:47.039
Or you can go, let's say, the cheaper way, you know,

00:54:47.159 --> 00:54:49.900
and to deploy multiple machines and then combine the RAM

00:54:49.900 --> 00:54:51.519
by connecting those machines over the network.

00:54:51.519 --> 00:54:55.699
There are advantages and disadvantages for running either a solution.

00:54:56.199 --> 00:55:01.559
For the computationally intensive applications, think about cryptocurrency mining.

00:55:02.239 --> 00:55:06.519
They needed devices like GPUs to do the calculations very fast.

00:55:07.119 --> 00:55:11.360
