00:57:11.800 --> 00:57:16.180
So, let's go ahead and continue talking about distributed systems.

00:57:16.179 --> 00:57:24.159
So I think if I remember right, in the lecture recording, we define what a distributed system is.

00:57:24.259 --> 00:57:29.619
So again, try to keep in mind this definition because everything that we talk ties back to this definition.

00:57:30.339 --> 00:57:37.139
So again, we say that a distributed system is a collection of independent computers that appears to its users as a single coherent system.

00:57:37.279 --> 00:57:38.839
It's two very important properties.

00:57:39.460 --> 00:57:41.199
Independent computers are a network.

00:57:41.199 --> 00:57:47.939
It means each computer has its own state, each computer has its own hardware operating system, software stack, right?

00:57:48.239 --> 00:57:50.699
And then we make them all work together, right?

00:57:50.759 --> 00:58:00.739
We connect them, we implement communication but also coordination between them so that they appear to the user as if it's one single system they access.

00:58:00.859 --> 00:58:04.399
And we do this through interfaces in the middleware.

00:58:05.480 --> 00:58:09.579
We also talked about the difference between decentralized and distributed systems, okay?

00:58:09.579 --> 00:58:19.539
So in quizzes and in the exam, if I ask you what is the definition of a distributed system, you go here and you pick this definition from Andrew Tannabal.

00:58:19.840 --> 00:58:28.920
Now, if I ask you when I want to highlight the difference between distributed and decentralized, you want to use the definitions from this slide.

00:58:29.119 --> 00:58:39.239
So we define decentralized system as a network computer system in which the processes and resources are necessarily spread.

00:58:39.579 --> 00:58:43.400
So they are distributed because of some sort of necessity.

00:58:44.079 --> 00:58:51.079
We gave, I think, as examples, federated learning where we have multiple hospitals, each having their own database.

00:58:52.159 --> 00:59:03.799
And the reason why we have a distributed system is because we need to interconnect this database in order to move the training algorithms to the databases because we cannot move the data because of HIPAA reasons.

00:59:04.240 --> 00:59:04.440
right?

00:59:04.440 --> 00:59:06.780
So because the data that is in those hospitals,

00:59:06.900 --> 00:59:07.180
right,

00:59:07.280 --> 00:59:08.360
pertain to people,

00:59:08.480 --> 00:59:08.600
right,

00:59:08.640 --> 00:59:09.160
to clients,

00:59:09.260 --> 00:59:10.019
to patients there,

00:59:10.280 --> 00:59:11.100
it needs to be protected.

00:59:11.240 --> 00:59:14.920
So it cannot simply be moved around to another data center to do AI

00:59:14.920 --> 00:59:15.300
training.

00:59:15.420 --> 00:59:18.600
So what we do is we move the AIs near the hospitals,

00:59:18.740 --> 00:59:19.519
we do training there,

00:59:19.560 --> 00:59:20.840
and then we aggregate the data.

00:59:21.180 --> 00:59:21.840
So we have,

00:59:21.980 --> 00:59:22.080
again,

00:59:22.100 --> 00:59:25.360
a system for doing this distributed AI learning,

00:59:25.519 --> 00:59:25.900
in a sense,

00:59:25.960 --> 00:59:26.820
or AI training,

00:59:27.140 --> 00:59:29.720
because we cannot move the data from those machines.

00:59:29.840 --> 00:59:30.680
So out of necessity,

00:59:30.680 --> 00:59:32.080
we have a distributed system.

00:59:32.079 --> 00:59:35.059
The same applies for blockchain technologies, okay?

00:59:35.539 --> 00:59:44.579
So, right, we wanted to build this distributive, right, ledger system because we cannot trust one bank with all of our transactions, right?

00:59:44.699 --> 00:59:54.500
We want to build it in a way where no one entity can control all of the transactions without having everyone be able to verify it, okay?

00:59:54.500 --> 01:00:02.619
And then a distributive system is defined as a network computer system in which processes and resources are sufficiently spread.

01:00:03.239 --> 01:00:05.139
So this is the case of, let's say, Gmail.

01:00:05.519 --> 01:00:15.219
When Google built the Gmail system, they initially built it for their own engineers so that they can communicate with each other and synchronize because they have an international team that was working on it.

01:00:15.840 --> 01:00:20.320
But then they decided to provide this service for everyone else for various reasons.

01:00:21.000 --> 01:00:25.660
So now all of a sudden they had to add more computers to their network computer system

01:00:25.660 --> 01:00:29.760
in order to be able to serve everyone the services behind Gmail.

01:00:30.300 --> 01:00:34.039
So out of sufficiency, they only added a sufficient number of computers

01:00:34.039 --> 01:00:39.539
in order to, for example, maybe provide Gmail to half of the population of the world.

01:00:40.300 --> 01:00:41.360
And that's where they stopped.

01:00:42.660 --> 01:00:42.920
Okay.

01:00:44.140 --> 01:00:46.880
We also talked about some misconceptions there.

01:00:47.039 --> 01:00:49.180
I'm not going to go into details here.

01:00:49.179 --> 01:00:50.519
the different perspectives

01:00:50.519 --> 01:00:52.659
which we study distributed systems.

