So after chapter one,

01:00:54.799 --> 01:00:56.219
we'll go into architectures

01:00:56.219 --> 01:00:58.839
and then we talked about the design.

01:00:59.099 --> 01:01:01.079
So similarly to the way,

01:01:01.359 --> 01:01:03.279
you know, whenever people build

01:01:03.279 --> 01:01:05.859
or build some sort of software

01:01:05.859 --> 01:01:07.839
or I'll give the example

01:01:07.839 --> 01:01:09.299
of operating systems, right?

01:01:09.319 --> 01:01:11.219
We have many operating systems out there,

01:01:11.659 --> 01:01:11.799
right?

01:01:11.799 --> 01:01:12.719
We have Windows,

01:01:13.019 --> 01:01:14.119
we have Mac OS,

01:01:14.619 --> 01:01:16.539
Android, iOS, right?

01:01:16.539 --> 01:01:19.360
Linux distributions.

01:01:20.219 --> 01:01:20.539
Right.

01:01:20.599 --> 01:01:23.900
And we have so many because they were all built for specific purposes.

01:01:24.239 --> 01:01:24.719
Right.

01:01:24.860 --> 01:01:26.539
In the end, an operating system.

01:01:26.659 --> 01:01:26.900
Right.

01:01:27.460 --> 01:01:29.440
It doesn't matter which one you pick.

01:01:29.460 --> 01:01:33.719
You know, it still implements some sort of general goal, but then they have been specialized.

01:01:34.719 --> 01:01:38.940
You know, can somebody tell me if you remember from your systems class, you know, what is

01:01:38.940 --> 01:01:40.619
the purpose of the operating system?

01:01:40.699 --> 01:01:41.400
Why do we need it?

01:01:42.039 --> 01:01:42.380
Yes.

01:01:42.380 --> 01:01:46.480
yes

01:01:46.480 --> 01:01:47.500
usability

01:01:47.500 --> 01:01:50.420
usability right so right

01:01:50.420 --> 01:01:52.480
the operating system acts as an

01:01:52.480 --> 01:01:54.519
interface right between human

01:01:54.519 --> 01:01:56.039
users and then the hardware

01:01:56.039 --> 01:01:58.440
right the hardware if you remember from your previous

01:01:58.440 --> 01:02:00.480
classes right uses these very

01:02:00.480 --> 01:02:01.920
weird protocols right

01:02:01.920 --> 01:02:04.240
machine instructions

01:02:04.240 --> 01:02:06.200
right which are very hard for humans to read

01:02:06.200 --> 01:02:08.300
and if you think about it right the way the

01:02:08.300 --> 01:02:10.340
computers are organized with CPU memory

01:02:10.340 --> 01:02:11.840
buses right

01:02:11.900 --> 01:02:20.019
Other devices, right, PCIe devices, I.O. devices, right, it would be very hard for a user to, like, keep everything in their minds of how do I use the system.

01:02:20.420 --> 01:02:26.320
So what the operating system does, it creates a set of abstractions that everyone can understand, right?

01:02:26.360 --> 01:02:30.260
Those abstractions are tied to the actual components of the computer, right?

01:02:30.280 --> 01:02:32.240
You have processes for CPU.

01:02:32.380 --> 01:02:37.019
So the programs that are running, right, we run them under the form of processes.

01:02:37.019 --> 01:02:43.219
And each process, right, has the impression that it's the only process using the processor, right, the CPU.

01:02:44.179 --> 01:02:45.719
We also have virtual memory.

01:02:45.860 --> 01:02:47.179
That's another abstraction, right?

01:02:47.340 --> 01:02:52.639
It also helps each program have the impression that they have access to the entire memory space of the computer.

01:02:53.219 --> 01:02:57.739
But in reality, right, that virtual memory gets translated to the physical addresses.

01:02:58.780 --> 01:03:03.300
We'll go back over it because we have a dedicated chapter on processes.

01:03:03.619 --> 01:03:05.860
So we'll go over those details again.

01:03:07.019 --> 01:03:17.380
And then in order to abstract the notion of input-output devices, in the case of the Linux operating system, they use the notion of files.

01:03:18.360 --> 01:03:22.699
So the mouse is a file, the keyboard is a file, and we can read data.

01:03:23.199 --> 01:03:25.739
The display is a file where we write data to it.

01:03:26.179 --> 01:03:31.539
So these are the three main abstractions that the operating system uses in order to build that interface.

01:03:31.539 --> 01:03:36.280
Now, like I said, we have many operating systems out there, Windows, macOS.

01:03:36.280 --> 01:03:45.620
So, for example, these operating systems were built with the goal of providing, again, a seamless, responsive interface.

01:03:46.500 --> 01:03:47.340
That's their purpose.

01:03:47.480 --> 01:03:53.660
They were built to, again, not be laggy, to be beautifully colored, intuitive for the users.

01:03:54.140 --> 01:03:54.920
That's their purpose.

01:03:55.080 --> 01:04:04.100
In the case of Linux distributions, the purpose was to build a very simple kernel that can help people learn about operating systems

01:04:04.099 --> 01:04:06.460
and you can learn people run programs

01:04:06.460 --> 01:04:10.039
without the overhead of the graphical user interfaces, for example.

01:04:10.579 --> 01:04:12.679
Or the overhead of other applications out there.

01:04:13.019 --> 01:04:16.980
This is one of the main reasons why Linux is very popular in distributed systems

01:04:16.980 --> 01:04:19.920
because it's so lightweight, but also because it's open source

01:04:19.920 --> 01:04:24.380
so people can tune it, can modify it, can make it serve whatever purpose they have.

01:04:24.920 --> 01:04:27.199
So, similarly to operating systems,

01:04:27.460 --> 01:04:29.360
whenever people build distributed systems,

01:04:29.860 --> 01:04:34.039
there are, of course, many goals that they try to achieve,

01:04:34.039 --> 01:04:36.300
what we did

01:04:36.300 --> 01:04:38.400
in the recording, I think we started

01:04:38.400 --> 01:04:40.059
talking about some of the general

01:04:40.059 --> 01:04:42.340
problems that can be found. The first

01:04:42.340 --> 01:04:44.460
one being the support

01:04:44.460 --> 01:04:46.179
for sharing of resources or why

01:04:46.179 --> 01:04:47.900
one of the main reasons we want to build

01:04:47.900 --> 01:04:50.079
distributed systems is because we have many

01:04:50.079 --> 01:04:52.039
computers and we want to figure out a way

01:04:52.039 --> 01:04:54.059
how do we use those resources efficiently?

01:04:54.239 --> 01:04:56.159
How do we give the user access to

01:04:56.159 --> 01:04:58.179
multiple computers simultaneously without

01:04:58.179 --> 01:05:00.219
maybe them being aware of those

01:05:00.219 --> 01:05:02.300
resources? Distribution transparency

01:05:02.300 --> 01:05:06.680
was another goal, so we wanted to hide away the fact that there are multiple computers.

01:05:07.380 --> 01:05:09.840
Google Drive does this very well.

01:05:09.920 --> 01:05:14.680
When you access a document in Google Drive, you don't know in which data center, in which

01:05:14.680 --> 01:05:18.380
server, on which hard drive in that server the data resides.

01:05:18.560 --> 01:05:20.380
It's done very transparently for you.

01:05:20.700 --> 01:05:24.140
It actually gives you the impression that those files actually reside on your computer.

01:05:24.920 --> 01:05:30.019
That's what Google tries to say even with Google Chrome and the Chromebooks.

01:05:30.420 --> 01:05:32.340
They want to say that, oh, you can have this computer

01:05:32.340 --> 01:05:34.699
that is always connected to the cloud, right?

01:05:34.759 --> 01:05:36.679
So it's as if you have all of the files locally,

01:05:36.820 --> 01:05:38.300
but actually they reside on the cloud

01:05:38.300 --> 01:05:40.139
and they're just cached on your computer.

01:05:40.759 --> 01:05:42.480
All right, then openness, of course, right?

01:05:42.639 --> 01:05:45.119
The openness goal refers to, again,

01:05:45.179 --> 01:05:46.900
building systems that are maintainable.

01:05:47.500 --> 01:05:48.800
You can extend them easily.

01:05:48.900 --> 01:05:50.759
They can easily interoperate with another system.

01:05:50.820 --> 01:05:51.639
That's very important.

01:05:51.639 --> 01:05:54.480
I think we talked about dependability as well.

01:05:54.480 --> 01:06:06.599
So the main concept there is that a distributed system is made out of multiple components.

