And actually, let me go to dependability so I can provide you another perspective on how to understand this concept.

01:06:16.380 --> 01:06:19.320
And I'm going to make a small parenthesis here.

01:06:19.320 --> 01:06:25.000
and I'm going to talk about engineering in the world of computers.

01:06:25.360 --> 01:06:27.400
And I'm not just referring to computer engineering

01:06:27.400 --> 01:06:30.320
because I know you can go and study computer engineering, right?

01:06:30.360 --> 01:06:33.080
And it refers to studying the hardware, right?

01:06:33.100 --> 01:06:34.720
And then you can do software engineering.

01:06:34.900 --> 01:06:37.480
So when I say engineering, I mean both of them, okay?

01:06:37.580 --> 01:06:41.680
So engineering is the task of solving problems

01:06:41.680 --> 01:06:44.780
or building products or applications, right?

01:06:44.820 --> 01:06:47.240
And they have a certain methodology that they follow.

01:06:48.059 --> 01:06:58.379
So in, let's say, software engineering, before they build anything, they have to actually define either what the problem is that they're solving or the product that they're building.

01:06:58.679 --> 01:07:00.859
So they do this by writing a specification.

01:07:01.679 --> 01:07:06.339
So once you're going to go, if you already have experience, then you already know what I'm talking about.

01:07:06.340 --> 01:07:08.260
but if they haven't worked yet for a company,

01:07:08.620 --> 01:07:10.820
you will see that one of the main important steps

01:07:10.820 --> 01:07:13.100
is that you need to work on a specification

01:07:13.100 --> 01:07:15.700
or a document that actually defines the product

01:07:15.700 --> 01:07:20.380
that you're creating or the problem that you are solving.

01:07:20.559 --> 01:07:23.079
So you spend a lot of time defining a document

01:07:23.079 --> 01:07:24.019
as best as you can.

01:07:24.539 --> 01:07:27.340
Then after that follows the software development

01:07:27.340 --> 01:07:29.079
or the implementation of the product

01:07:29.079 --> 01:07:31.480
or the implementation of the application

01:07:31.480 --> 01:07:33.340
that solves the problem, right?

01:07:33.400 --> 01:07:34.700
So that's the fun part.

01:07:34.820 --> 01:07:35.940
We all like to do it, right?

01:07:35.940 --> 01:07:38.220
AIs can do it 50 times faster than us.

01:07:38.300 --> 01:07:39.059
You know, that's all great.

01:07:39.159 --> 01:07:40.480
That was never really a problem.

01:07:41.440 --> 01:07:47.019
But then the next challenge is making sure that the software that was created actually

01:07:47.019 --> 01:07:48.920
works according to that specification.

01:07:49.579 --> 01:07:54.780
So a lot of the time we spend it actually in doing testing or verification to make sure

01:07:54.780 --> 01:07:57.500
that the application works according to its specification.

01:07:58.700 --> 01:08:03.880
Okay, so when it comes to distributed systems, they also have a specification as well.

01:08:03.880 --> 01:08:12.940
Now, the only, let's say, complicating thing is that distributed systems have many components that are connected in very interesting ways.

01:08:13.880 --> 01:08:24.760
And let's say the distributed system itself or the services, they actually depend on those components working well.

01:08:25.220 --> 01:08:29.279
And for those components to actually work also according to their own specification.

01:08:29.279 --> 01:08:41.559
So we say that a distributed system, it's a big system that is made out of multiple components, but that the distributed system depends on the correctness of those components that it's made of.

01:08:41.560 --> 01:08:59.860
All right. So, dependability refers to building systems that, again, work well and function well because, again, the system itself depends on other components.

01:09:00.560 --> 01:09:07.140
And I think what we did in the lecture recording, I talked about some of the metrics that people use to measure dependability.

01:09:07.680 --> 01:09:10.100
You can go and just rewatch the lecture for these.

01:09:10.100 --> 01:09:14.700
But the concept itself is that, again, a distributive system is made out of multiple components

01:09:14.700 --> 01:09:19.140
and the correctness of the distributive system depends on the correctness of those components.

01:09:20.660 --> 01:09:25.740
We sometimes see this as well even when you do software engineering with small programs.

01:09:25.880 --> 01:09:29.820
You will be using, for example, the standard library to implement applications.

01:09:30.579 --> 01:09:35.980
So the correctness of your program also depends on the correctness of the implementation of those standard libraries.

01:09:35.979 --> 01:09:43.719
And that's why I want you to stay with the standard libraries and not use any other package that you find on the internet because they might have bugs.

01:09:44.199 --> 01:09:46.259
And those bugs will trickle in your application.

01:09:46.819 --> 01:09:48.299
So they will create bugs in your application.

01:09:48.839 --> 01:09:51.500
So the same principle applies but at a larger scale.

01:09:52.879 --> 01:09:53.079
All right.

