The way that I choose this class is usually one hour and a half, we take a 15-minute break, and then we come back for almost an hour and a half.

01:10:13.439 --> 01:10:19.879
So, security, it's another general goal when it comes to implementing distributed systems.

01:10:20.479 --> 01:10:26.759
It makes sense, because a distributed system is a collection of independent computers that are connected over a network.

01:10:27.139 --> 01:10:32.139
When we send data over the network, if we're not very careful, we might send the data over plain text.

01:10:32.140 --> 01:10:38.800
If our distributed system is geographically distributed, we might send plain text data over the internet.

01:10:38.940 --> 01:10:44.000
So somebody could intercept it and steal the data and then, you know, create security breaches in our system.

01:10:44.760 --> 01:10:51.660
So it's very important, right, that we secure the system and that we secure the components that makes up the distributed system.

01:10:52.020 --> 01:10:59.560
We can also say, right, that a distributed system that is not secure, right, can be compromised, which then is not dependable anymore.

01:10:59.560 --> 01:11:02.780
So it can also affect the dependability goal as well.

01:11:03.960 --> 01:11:08.140
Now, I just want to make another small parenthesis here.

01:11:08.600 --> 01:11:14.980
In this class, the only extent that we will focus when it comes to security

01:11:14.980 --> 01:11:19.660
will be what's covered in the introductory chapter, so in the first chapter.

01:11:20.320 --> 01:11:24.039
There are a lot of classes offered at default in terms of cybersecurity,

01:11:24.520 --> 01:11:28.039
computer security that you can take where you'll learn way more many things

01:11:28.039 --> 01:11:32.539
that are in this class about how to secure computers, networks, and clusters of computers.

01:11:32.859 --> 01:11:37.500
But I just want to highlight some of the more important, let's say, mechanisms and technologies

01:11:37.500 --> 01:11:39.859
that are relevant for distributed systems.

01:11:40.779 --> 01:11:46.560
So when it comes to security, what we typically want to implement is two things.

01:11:46.699 --> 01:11:49.500
Now, again, if you've already taken a computer security class,

01:11:49.739 --> 01:11:50.699
you're going to contradict me.

01:11:50.779 --> 01:11:52.000
There's other things that you want.

01:11:52.579 --> 01:11:56.439
But for the purpose of distributed systems, let's just look at these two things.

01:11:56.439 --> 01:12:00.000
So we want to implement typically confidentiality and integrity.

01:12:01.000 --> 01:12:07.239
Confidentiality refers to disclosing information only to authorized parties, right?

01:12:07.279 --> 01:12:11.139
So only the people that are authorized to access the information can access the information.

01:12:11.759 --> 01:12:19.159
And then integrity refers to ensuring that alterations to assets of a system can be made only in an authorized way.

01:12:19.159 --> 01:12:27.859
So, iterations to, you know, the configuration of a system to data, information, structure, right, can all be done in an authorized way.

01:12:28.420 --> 01:12:36.260
So, to do this, when it comes to security, in order to achieve confidentiality and integrity, we usually use three mechanisms.

01:12:36.819 --> 01:12:40.639
So, these are three abstract mechanisms that can be implemented in various ways.

01:12:41.920 --> 01:12:44.220
Authentication, of course, is a very important mechanism.

01:12:44.220 --> 01:12:49.140
authentication refers to verifying the correctness of a claim identity.

01:12:49.500 --> 01:12:51.980
So for example, whenever you're connecting to a remote machine,

01:12:52.280 --> 01:12:54.920
including your virtual machine, you have to authenticate first.

01:12:55.440 --> 01:12:58.060
And you're using the username-password combo.

01:12:58.699 --> 01:13:02.280
You can also use, if you want, username and public-private key combo as well

01:13:02.280 --> 01:13:03.280
to implement authentication.

01:13:04.020 --> 01:13:06.480
There are various ways authentication can be implemented

01:13:06.480 --> 01:13:09.900
in order to achieve, let's say, a high degree of security.

01:13:10.980 --> 01:13:13.060
Another mechanism is authorization.

01:13:13.680 --> 01:13:20.920
So authorization refers to verifying if an entity has the proper access rights.

01:13:21.420 --> 01:13:23.260
You've already encountered this as well.

01:13:23.340 --> 01:13:27.520
Well, once you start working on part one and part two, you will encounter it.

01:13:27.640 --> 01:13:31.800
Basically, a form of authorization could be file permissions.

01:13:32.120 --> 01:13:39.580
So the file permissions can be used to say which entity is allowed to access which resources in what way.

01:13:40.239 --> 01:13:43.420
There's also access control lists, role-based access control.

01:13:43.420 --> 01:13:47.260
There's all different kinds of forms that are very popular, especially in web applications.

01:13:48.479 --> 01:13:51.140
And then lastly, the last mechanism is trust.

01:13:52.119 --> 01:14:04.619
So trust refers to the property that an entity can be assured that another will perform particular actions according to a specific expectation.

01:14:04.619 --> 01:14:12.760
So this means that, let's say that I have a username called AORHian and I'm logging into a server, right?

01:14:13.119 --> 01:14:20.859
The server will assume that the user that logged in as AORHian, right, is the actual user that is supposed to access that machine.

01:14:21.000 --> 01:14:30.939
If somebody steals my credentials and logs in as AORHian, right, then they can go and do the things that I can do, but they can also do malicious things on the machine as well.

01:14:31.179 --> 01:14:43.179
So, right, so trust, again, refers to how do we verify to make sure that, you know, an entity that says who they are, you know, can access the machine and can work according to their, let's say, expectations, right?

01:14:43.180 --> 01:14:47.720
if somebody steals my credentials, they're probably going to do things very differently than what I'm doing.

01:14:47.800 --> 01:14:49.760
They're going to defer in terms of a pattern of access.

01:14:50.159 --> 01:14:54.100
So there are tools out there that can actually monitor this kind of access

01:14:54.100 --> 01:14:58.960
and then can identify if somebody, you know, does things that they're not supposed to do in the system

01:14:58.960 --> 01:15:01.600
or that it's different from their typical behavior.

01:15:02.560 --> 01:15:05.480
But usually, in practice, trust is very hard to implement

01:15:05.480 --> 01:15:08.820
because you don't have complete information.

01:15:08.980 --> 01:15:12.460
And because of the nature of how we authenticate to the machines, it's very hard.

01:15:12.460 --> 01:15:26.760
I think people nowadays, they try to tie, for example, authentication to biometric information like, you know, fingerprints, you know, that way they will give you a mechanism that is going to be harder to trick in a sense.

01:15:27.220 --> 01:15:33.180
But in the end, this is all digital computers, you know, so it's very hard to build a perfect system.

01:15:34.260 --> 01:15:34.460
Okay.

01:15:34.720 --> 01:15:38.859
And that's why people usually focus when it comes to trust on detecting unusual behavior.

01:15:40.119 --> 01:15:42.039
Of course, these are abstract mechanisms.

01:15:42.039 --> 01:15:50.460
But when it comes to the actual practical mechanisms or implementation security, the most popular ones, of course, is using encryption and decryption.

01:15:50.979 --> 01:15:58.140
Can somebody tell me, you know, using your own words, especially if you've already taken these classes, you know, what is encryption?

01:15:58.439 --> 01:16:03.159
Or how would you explain, you know, to somebody from Kidding Carter what encryption does?

01:16:04.119 --> 01:16:04.600
Yes.

01:16:05.140 --> 01:16:05.859
What is your name?

01:16:07.079 --> 01:16:08.019
Tarak, you said?

01:16:08.519 --> 01:16:08.960
Okay, fair.

01:16:12.039 --> 01:16:19.300
plain text and converting it into like non-human readable format And why do we wanna do this?

01:16:19.300 --> 01:16:27.859
Yeah, they cannot extract the information, right so again if you want to for example

01:16:27.859 --> 01:16:34.439
send data from one person to another, we wanna make sure that nobody can read that data so

01:16:34.439 --> 01:16:38.960
what we do is we do some sort of encryption, right if we wanna store data somewhere and

01:16:38.960 --> 01:16:41.960
Only the person that stored the data can access that data.

01:16:42.079 --> 01:16:43.039
We also use encryption.

01:16:43.340 --> 01:16:49.560
So, again, it's a mechanism that allows us to transform data, like you said, from plain text,

01:16:49.800 --> 01:16:53.560
where all of the information is readable, to some sort of ciphertext,

01:16:53.899 --> 01:16:57.840
where basically there's no leak of the information from the original plain text data.

01:16:58.619 --> 01:17:02.960
The way encryption and decryption systems work is by using, of course, keys.

01:17:03.619 --> 01:17:06.359
Keys can be implemented in various ways.

01:17:06.359 --> 01:17:10.359
a lot of times they follow some sort of mathematical formula to generate some numbers

01:17:10.359 --> 01:17:14.319
that then can be used to implement encryption and decryption algorithms.

01:17:15.899 --> 01:17:24.359
Now when it comes to encryption, there are two types of, let's say, encryption schemas out there.

01:17:24.359 --> 01:17:29.199
Symmetric key encryption schemas and then asymmetric key encryption schemas.

01:17:29.199 --> 01:17:39.399
symmetric key encryption schemas are a type of schemas that use the same key to do both encryption and decryption

01:17:39.399 --> 01:17:42.300
that key is typically called a secret key

01:17:42.300 --> 01:17:47.119
it's a key that you generate once and then you can use it to both encrypt and decrypt the data

01:17:47.439 --> 01:17:50.059
so basically you have two separate functions

01:17:50.059 --> 01:17:53.699
an E function for encryption, a D function for decryption

01:17:53.699 --> 01:17:57.699
and you can apply encryption and decryption over some sort of data

01:17:57.699 --> 01:17:59.439
using the key K.

01:18:00.000 --> 01:18:02.880
And the property is that if you have a piece of data

01:18:02.880 --> 01:18:06.739
and you encrypt it with a key K and then you decrypt that result,

01:18:06.960 --> 01:18:08.679
it should give you back the original data.

01:18:11.099 --> 01:18:15.300
There are many symmetric key encryption schemas out there

01:18:15.300 --> 01:18:16.300
that have been created.

01:18:16.539 --> 01:18:19.079
They're very powerful ones that are very hard to break

01:18:19.079 --> 01:18:22.340
even for the realm of quantum computing, let's say.

01:18:23.699 --> 01:18:31.579
So however, there is one main disadvantage and the main drawback of the system doesn't have to do with the algorithms used behind.

01:18:31.720 --> 01:18:34.880
It has more to do with how do we disseminate this key?

01:18:35.019 --> 01:18:37.559
How do we transfer this key from one entity to another?

01:18:37.880 --> 01:18:43.359
So let's say that I want to talk with my friend Lucian, right?

01:18:43.359 --> 01:19:12.579
And in order to communicate with him, you know, in an encrypted fashion, we would generate a secret key, but I need to transfer that key to my friend, Gucci, so then if I transfer it, for example, over the internet, right, if somebody is, you know, monitoring the traffic and, you know, sniffing the traffic, let's say, you know, they can intercept my key, steal my key, and then they can also decrypt and encrypt, you know, the messages using the exact same thing.

01:19:12.979 --> 01:19:14.859
So this is one of the main challenges.

01:19:15.000 --> 01:19:19.899
How do we send those keys in a very secure way?

01:19:20.539 --> 01:19:26.979
Some people might want to say that, well, you can put the key on a flash drive and you can drive there to your friend and drop the key there.

01:19:27.180 --> 01:19:32.559
But if they live in Australia and I'm in the US, then that would be a very expensive flight just to share some keys.

01:19:33.059 --> 01:19:37.319
So this is why people started thinking about other ways to build encryption.

01:19:38.139 --> 01:19:41.639
and this takes us to the second kind of encryption schema,

01:19:41.799 --> 01:19:43.739
which is the asymmetric key encryption schema.

01:19:44.299 --> 01:19:48.199
So in asymmetric, instead of using one single key,

01:19:49.039 --> 01:19:50.960
these systems, they use two keys.

01:19:51.359 --> 01:19:53.759
They use a public key and a private key,

01:19:53.880 --> 01:19:55.939
or sometimes also called a secret key as well.

01:19:57.019 --> 01:19:59.939
So in these key encryption schemas,

01:19:59.939 --> 01:20:02.739
we, of course, still use mathematical models

01:20:02.739 --> 01:20:06.319
to generate now two numbers that are related to each other,

01:20:07.059 --> 01:20:10.880
And the interesting relation is that if you have a piece of data,

01:20:11.500 --> 01:20:15.399
you can either encrypt or decrypt the data using either of the keys.

01:20:15.880 --> 01:20:18.179
But if you use one of the keys to encrypt the data,

01:20:18.279 --> 01:20:20.439
you have to use the other key to decrypt the data.

01:20:20.880 --> 01:20:24.259
So if I'm using, for example, the secret key to encrypt the data,

01:20:24.599 --> 01:20:26.899
I need to then use the public key to decrypt the data.

01:20:27.319 --> 01:20:29.639
But I can also use the public key to encrypt the data,

01:20:29.639 --> 01:20:32.659
which means that when I apply the secret key,

01:20:33.219 --> 01:20:36.000
the algorithm with the secret key should give me back the data.

01:20:36.000 --> 01:20:37.020
They should decrypt the data.

01:20:37.920 --> 01:20:43.680
So this can be expressed under the form here.

01:20:43.979 --> 01:20:46.739
If you ignore this bullet point, right,

01:20:46.779 --> 01:20:50.340
if I have a piece of data and I encrypt it using the public key,

01:20:50.439 --> 01:20:55.560
let's say, of Bob, and then I apply again the decryption algorithm

01:20:55.560 --> 01:20:59.100
using the secret key of Bob, I should get back the same data.

01:21:01.100 --> 01:21:05.920
So what's very interesting about this public-private key encryption scheme

01:21:05.920 --> 01:21:09.600
is that it actually bypasses that security issue

01:21:09.600 --> 01:21:12.960
that symmetric key encryption schema keys have.

01:21:13.460 --> 01:21:17.720
So now, if, for example, I want to share the key with somebody else

01:21:17.720 --> 01:21:19.279
so I can implement secure communication,

01:21:19.619 --> 01:21:21.279
I can share within the public key,

01:21:21.600 --> 01:21:24.319
and even if the malicious entity has the public key,

01:21:24.659 --> 01:21:25.920
the system will still work,

01:21:26.000 --> 01:21:29.659
as long as I keep my secret key secret or my private key private.

01:21:32.000 --> 01:21:32.640
All right.

01:21:32.640 --> 01:21:33.980
So, for example, here,

01:21:33.979 --> 01:21:36.759
Let's see if Alice wants to send a message to Bob.

01:21:37.500 --> 01:21:43.459
What Alice needs to do, Alice needs to get a copy of the public key from Bob.

01:21:43.839 --> 01:21:51.699
Once it has access to that key, Alice can encrypt the message that he wants to send to Bob using Bob's public key.

01:21:52.199 --> 01:21:54.359
Kind of similar to the analogy that I made earlier.

01:21:54.639 --> 01:22:01.899
So if Alice wants to send a message, he needs to grab a lock from Bob, puts the message in the mailbox, locks it with a lock.

01:22:01.899 --> 01:22:04.219
so it encrypts the data using Bob's puppy key.

01:22:04.519 --> 01:22:08.899
And then since Bob is the only entity that has access to the private key of Bob,

01:22:09.000 --> 01:22:10.299
only Bob can decrypt that data.

01:22:10.960 --> 01:22:12.199
So when Bob receives the message,

01:22:12.319 --> 01:22:17.079
he can use the secret key from Bob to decrypt the data

01:22:17.079 --> 01:22:21.759
and he has access to whatever message Alice sent to Bob.

01:22:22.319 --> 01:22:24.519
Now, there's also some problems here as well.

01:22:25.059 --> 01:22:27.159
So one of the main problems that appears

01:22:27.159 --> 01:22:33.159
is that how do we guarantee that if a message is sent by someone,

01:22:33.420 --> 01:22:34.180
let's say from Alice,

01:22:34.599 --> 01:22:37.199
how do we guarantee that when I decrypt the message

01:22:37.199 --> 01:22:39.079
that the message actually came from Alice

01:22:39.079 --> 01:22:41.340
and not from somebody impersonating Alice?

01:22:41.960 --> 01:22:44.639
So let's say I have Trudy there, right,

01:22:44.639 --> 01:22:48.559
who also gets a copy of Bob's public key somehow, right,

01:22:48.619 --> 01:22:51.500
and they encrypt the message and they send the message before Alice does.

01:22:51.939 --> 01:22:56.399
How does Bob know then that the message came from Trudy and not Alice?

01:22:56.719 --> 01:23:10.179
So for this, we need to figure out a way to implement a very old technique that was used, you know, even during medieval times, which is the ability to sign the messages, right?

01:23:10.279 --> 01:23:13.719
So you sign them in a way where you can validate where the message came from.

01:23:13.720 --> 01:23:30.600
The way people used to do this during medieval times, right, if a lord wanted to write, you know, to another king, you know, a message, they would write a letter, put it in, write an envelope, and then seal the envelope with some sort of stamp and wax, right, and then somebody broke that envelope, it means, oh, somebody tampered with the message.

01:23:31.100 --> 01:23:41.360
But if, you know, the seal was still there, then it means nobody tampered, and by looking at the seal, they were very, you know, intricate, you know, the seals, we can validate who actually sent that seal.

01:23:41.359 --> 01:23:55.399
So in digital systems we can do the same thing by actually using both pairs of public private keys from both Alice and Bob.

01:23:55.399 --> 01:24:02.019
So how do we attach some sort of signature to the message?

01:24:02.179 --> 01:24:05.859
Well let's say again Alice wants to send a message to Bob.

01:24:06.259 --> 01:24:09.960
Alice will get a copy of Bob's public key.

01:24:09.960 --> 01:24:14.480
But Alice will also share her public key with Bob as well.

01:24:14.980 --> 01:24:17.720
So now they both have access to each other's public keys.

01:24:18.420 --> 01:24:23.340
So what Alice will do, Alice will prepare a message.

01:24:23.779 --> 01:24:28.880
The message will contain the actual message or the data that Alice wants to send to Bob.

01:24:29.480 --> 01:24:35.300
And then in addition to the data, it will also contain another ciphertext.

01:24:35.300 --> 01:24:42.020
And the ciphertext is obtained by encrypting the data using Alice's secret key.

01:24:43.079 --> 01:24:48.340
All right, so we send the data, the data encrypted using Alice's secret key.

01:24:48.560 --> 01:24:54.239
And then what we do is we encrypt this entire bigger message using Bob's public key.

01:24:55.239 --> 01:24:58.840
Right, so we have a huge, you know, a blob encrypted.

01:24:59.000 --> 01:24:59.840
We send it to Bob.

01:24:59.840 --> 01:25:06.119
Now Bob has access to Alice's public key, but also to his own secret key.

01:25:06.659 --> 01:25:11.960
So what we do is by using Bob's secret key, we decrypt the message.

01:25:12.640 --> 01:25:15.520
We can see the data that was sent by Alice.

01:25:16.119 --> 01:25:25.720
And then what we do is over the ciphertext that was sent by Alice, we apply the public key of Alice.

01:25:26.640 --> 01:25:34.699
And if we do that, this would be the equivalent of encrypting the data using Alice's secret key

01:25:34.699 --> 01:25:39.140
and then decrypting the data using Alice's public key should give us back a copy of the data.

01:25:39.820 --> 01:25:44.920
And as long as these two pieces of data are the same, it means that the data came from Alice.

01:25:45.880 --> 01:25:51.500
This is what SSH is using when you're setting public-private key-based authentication.

01:25:52.119 --> 01:25:54.480
You are copying your public key on the server.

01:25:54.939 --> 01:25:59.599
When you use SSH, the server automatically copies a public key in your program

01:25:59.599 --> 01:26:02.779
and then they exchange some messages between each other

01:26:02.779 --> 01:26:05.519
and that's how authentication is achieved, right?

01:26:05.619 --> 01:26:13.519
Using the exact same, let's say, message signing mechanism that we saw here.

01:26:14.259 --> 01:26:14.919
Okay?

01:26:15.459 --> 01:26:22.059
So, to implement, again, secure communication with validation of authenticity, right?

01:26:22.060 --> 01:26:25.620
We implement signatures basically on both machines.

01:26:25.740 --> 01:26:26.840
We generate public, public keys.

01:26:27.000 --> 01:26:29.000
We share the public keys between the two machines.

01:26:29.600 --> 01:26:35.320
And then we send a few challenging messages to validate that the encryption is done successfully.

01:26:35.580 --> 01:26:41.220
And when it's done, it means that we have valid authentication and we can have a secure channel for sending here.

01:26:41.820 --> 01:26:44.060
Now, there's also another problem here.

01:26:44.960 --> 01:26:51.000
So the problem is if we want to sign the messages by sending the data

01:26:51.000 --> 01:26:54.300
and then also an encrypted variant of the data,

01:26:54.460 --> 01:26:57.840
where basically whenever we have to send, let's say, one gigabyte of data,

01:26:57.940 --> 01:27:01.440
we need to duplicate that data because we have to send the encrypted version.

01:27:01.440 --> 01:27:06.880
So we will send one gigabyte of data, which will be encrypted using Bob's public key,

01:27:07.220 --> 01:27:09.620
but then another one gigabyte of data,

01:27:09.619 --> 01:27:14.659
which will be encrypted using Alice's secret key plus Bob's public key as well.

01:27:15.300 --> 01:27:19.300
So this doubles the amount of data that I have to send for each message,

01:27:19.739 --> 01:27:20.800
which is not very efficient.

01:27:20.960 --> 01:27:25.819
So we want to implement this in a little bit more efficient way.

01:27:26.380 --> 01:27:29.579
So this takes us to the second practical mechanism

01:27:29.579 --> 01:27:33.439
that is used to implement security, especially in distributed systems,

01:27:33.460 --> 01:27:34.840
which is secure hashing.

01:27:35.779 --> 01:27:38.699
Now, before I talk about secure hashing,

01:27:38.699 --> 01:27:41.779
I know we talked about hash tables earlier.

