01:27:45.300 --> 01:27:46.739
But what is a hash function?

01:27:47.380 --> 01:27:49.840
Or what are the properties of a hash function?

01:27:51.319 --> 01:27:51.599
Yes.

01:27:51.779 --> 01:27:55.019
It runs some mathematical operation over the data

01:27:55.019 --> 01:27:59.380
to yield a unique identifier or value.

01:28:00.099 --> 01:28:01.239
Unique identifier, yeah.

01:28:01.340 --> 01:28:01.939
That's good.

01:28:02.260 --> 01:28:05.159
So it does some transformation from an input to an output.

01:28:05.159 --> 01:28:07.380
What can you tell me about the input and the output?

01:28:08.699 --> 01:28:14.880
The inputs usually transform with like a prime number.

01:28:14.880 --> 01:28:20.199
The hash function uses a prime number to transform the input from the input to an output.

01:28:20.199 --> 01:28:25.519
But I was interested more in terms of the size of the input and the output.

01:28:25.519 --> 01:28:29.059
Usually it hashes to like something smaller and it always gives the same output?

01:28:29.059 --> 01:28:33.220
Yes, or not the same sized output but not the same output.

01:28:33.220 --> 01:28:38.460
So a very important property of a hash function is that it can receive a variable-sized input,

01:28:38.840 --> 01:28:41.440
but it always generates a fixed-size output.

01:28:42.020 --> 01:28:43.579
So it does a transformation.

01:28:44.760 --> 01:28:49.460
Now, let's say if I know the output of a hash function,

01:28:49.920 --> 01:28:52.600
can I determine which input generated that output?

01:28:53.420 --> 01:28:54.420
Why not?

01:28:54.420 --> 01:29:10.619
So because the hash function, the output which we are getting is not the data which we input.

01:29:10.619 --> 01:29:16.440
It's just the mathematical function or the value which it calculated.

01:29:16.619 --> 01:29:20.859
So we cannot go back towards the data which we gave in that code.

01:29:21.560 --> 01:29:22.819
I hope it makes sense.

