01:29:31.800 --> 01:29:32.939
We don't trust it.

01:29:33.380 --> 01:29:36.119
So actually, there's an even simpler explanation, right?

01:29:36.180 --> 01:29:38.920
So think about in mathematical terms.

01:29:39.079 --> 01:29:42.199
They call it, you know, one-to-one correspondence, right?

01:29:42.479 --> 01:29:45.119
So the hash function, they don't have one-to-one correspondence.

01:29:45.760 --> 01:29:51.239
So the input space is infinite, because you can have variable size inputs, right?

01:29:51.279 --> 01:29:53.260
There's no limit on how big an input can be.

01:29:53.260 --> 01:29:57.020
Of course, if you have something that is super large, you won't be able to do the calculation on time.

01:29:57.300 --> 01:30:01.820
But technically, algorithmically, the input can be of any size, right?

01:30:01.820 --> 01:30:05.239
So you have an infinite space of input data points, right?

01:30:05.260 --> 01:30:07.760
But the output space is fixed in size.

01:30:08.280 --> 01:30:14.119
So there's a discrete number of data points that can be mapped from the input to the output

01:30:14.119 --> 01:30:15.520
whenever you apply a hash function.

01:30:16.100 --> 01:30:18.440
So what happens, and I'm sure you've heard about this,

01:30:18.539 --> 01:30:21.300
when you build a hash table, right, you have collisions.

01:30:21.300 --> 01:30:24.640
when multiple inputs actually lead you to the same output.

01:30:25.739 --> 01:30:27.860
So now, if you have an output,

01:30:28.320 --> 01:30:30.659
in order to determine which input generated,

01:30:30.659 --> 01:30:33.020
you actually have an infinite number of inputs

01:30:33.020 --> 01:30:35.079
that could have been mapped to that output.

01:30:36.000 --> 01:30:38.460
So that's why it's computationally impossible

01:30:38.460 --> 01:30:41.520
to actually find what input.

01:30:41.760 --> 01:30:43.980
You might have a chance to find it,

01:30:44.140 --> 01:30:46.560
but you'll have to iterate over all possible values,

01:30:46.680 --> 01:30:48.060
and there's an infinite number of values

01:30:48.060 --> 01:30:51.120
that could have a collision on the same output.

01:30:51.900 --> 01:30:56.180
Okay, so secure hash functions have the same properties

01:30:56.180 --> 01:30:58.039
with one additional property.

01:30:58.600 --> 01:31:03.800
What we want with secure hash functions is we want a hash function

01:31:03.800 --> 01:31:08.760
that if we apply it on two input data points,

01:31:09.240 --> 01:31:11.620
and these data points only differ by one bit,

01:31:11.760 --> 01:31:14.260
we want the output data to be very different.

01:31:15.239 --> 01:31:19.760
That's the extra, let's say, property that secure hash functions have.

01:31:20.320 --> 01:31:22.860
Okay, so this is expressed here by this first bullet point.

01:31:22.960 --> 01:31:26.940
Any change from data to data star will lead to a completely different string

01:31:26.940 --> 01:31:33.680
when you apply the hash function h over data star when compared to h over data.

01:31:34.500 --> 01:31:39.039
Okay, otherwise, again, it receives as an input variable size.

01:31:39.199 --> 01:31:41.460
It returns as an output fixed length, okay?

01:31:41.460 --> 01:31:46.899
and then it still has that property that if we know the output and the hash function,

01:31:46.980 --> 01:31:50.140
it's very hard to determine which input generated the hash function.

01:31:50.359 --> 01:31:54.920
So using hash functions, we can now implement practical digital signatures

01:31:54.920 --> 01:31:58.159
and this is what modern-day systems actually use.

01:31:59.199 --> 01:32:02.039
So let's say again Alice wants to send a message to Bob.

01:32:02.279 --> 01:32:04.100
We exchange the public keys, right?

01:32:04.100 --> 01:32:07.380
And now Alice prepares a packet here,

01:32:07.380 --> 01:32:12.000
prepares a packet that contains the message or the data that Alice wants to send to Bob,

01:32:12.460 --> 01:32:17.480
the hash function that Alice wants to use, and of course the signature.

01:32:17.699 --> 01:32:23.060
And the signature, we will calculate it as the hash function applied over the data.

01:32:23.819 --> 01:32:29.100
And then we encrypt that result, that output, using Alice's secret key.

01:32:29.579 --> 01:32:34.960
And then we encrypt this entire packet using Bob's public key, send the message to Bob.

01:32:35.399 --> 01:32:39.039
Bob will be able to decrypt the packet using Bob's secret key.

01:32:39.819 --> 01:32:39.980
Right?

01:32:40.239 --> 01:32:41.720
Bob will be able to read the data,

01:32:42.199 --> 01:32:44.359
will be able to read the hash function from the packet.

01:32:44.779 --> 01:32:45.039
Right?

01:32:45.100 --> 01:32:50.279
And then what Bob needs to do is he needs to decrypt the signature

01:32:50.279 --> 01:32:51.939
using Alice's public key.

01:32:52.279 --> 01:32:55.039
And then he needs to recalculate that hash output

01:32:55.159 --> 01:32:57.920
by applying that hash function over the data.

01:32:58.300 --> 01:33:00.659
And if those two results are the same,

01:33:00.960 --> 01:33:04.000
it means that the message actually came from Alice.

01:33:04.000 --> 01:33:08.479
So we have, again, practical digital signatures, right?

01:33:08.579 --> 01:33:12.460
The hash function, the output of the hash function is smaller, right?

