01:58:29.840 --> 01:58:43.020
All right, let's get back to distributive systems.

01:58:43.020 --> 01:58:45.539
We'll see, maybe we can finish a little bit further.

01:58:45.539 --> 01:58:48.319
But anyways, we talked about various goals.

01:58:48.319 --> 01:58:53.900
The last goal that I want to cover is scale or scalability.

01:58:53.899 --> 01:58:57.719
And a lot of the times, this is actually one of the main reasons

01:58:57.719 --> 01:59:00.000
why people don't build distributive systems

01:59:00.000 --> 01:59:03.619
is because they want to improve the performance of an application

01:59:03.619 --> 01:59:08.119
or they want to be able to provide a service to a lot of people.

01:59:08.920 --> 01:59:11.779
So, and to better understand this notion of scalability,

01:59:12.179 --> 01:59:15.739
I think we have to understand a little bit the evolution of computers,

01:59:16.299 --> 01:59:18.779
especially in the area of supercomputers.

01:59:18.779 --> 01:59:21.059
Because distributive systems actually,

01:59:21.060 --> 01:59:24.500
or result, a byproduct of a different approach

01:59:24.500 --> 01:59:26.200
for building a supercomputer.

01:59:26.860 --> 01:59:30.960
So again, I'll take you way back before I was born,

01:59:31.580 --> 01:59:32.920
all right, 50, 60 years ago

01:59:32.920 --> 01:59:34.840
when people were building computers, right,

01:59:34.980 --> 01:59:37.260
all of the computers were single node,

01:59:37.440 --> 01:59:40.160
single processor, single core, right,

01:59:40.360 --> 01:59:41.720
one instruction at a time

01:59:41.720 --> 01:59:43.820
and they were looking at how do we make things faster.

01:59:43.820 --> 01:59:45.980
There were various techniques that they used.

01:59:46.100 --> 01:59:49.440
They either made the clock of the computer faster,

01:59:49.439 --> 01:59:53.500
So how many instructions per second are being executed by the processor?

01:59:53.899 --> 01:59:57.739
They looked at various kinds of parallelism, including instruction parallelism.

01:59:57.839 --> 01:59:59.199
And we'll see what this is later.

02:00:00.519 --> 02:00:05.159
But the key aspect was that the computers were not connected over any network.

02:00:05.339 --> 02:00:08.259
This came very late in the history of computers.

02:00:08.979 --> 02:00:14.139
And the powerful computers were huge, were sometimes as big as this room.

02:00:14.919 --> 02:00:17.919
They had a lot of redundancy built in and a lot of fast hardware.

02:00:17.919 --> 02:00:19.239
They were very expensive.

02:00:19.439 --> 02:00:39.699
So once people started developing network technology to connect our computers into a network, some people decided to explore to see what would happen if we were to, instead of buying a very expensive computer that costs tens of millions of dollars,

02:00:39.699 --> 02:00:51.439
What if we were to buy a bunch of computers that cost less than a million dollars and then just interconnect them over a network and then program the application in a way where it uses all of these compute nodes.

02:00:52.119 --> 02:00:56.179
And that was the first, let's say, computer cluster that was built.

02:00:56.399 --> 02:01:05.359
And it proved that you can actually build computers that can run fast while also maintaining, let's say, a low budget.

02:01:06.079 --> 02:01:08.399
Right. And they built it using commodity hardware.

02:01:08.399 --> 02:01:11.879
So they built it using desktop computers that you can go and buy off the shelf.

02:01:12.339 --> 02:01:18.799
They just interconnected them and implemented, again, the applications in a way where they knew how to use these resources.

02:01:19.699 --> 02:01:26.000
So that's where, again, the notion of distributed system came to be, so to these parallel computers.

02:01:27.039 --> 02:01:35.839
And what people started asking themselves very early on is, okay, if we add more computers, does the performance of the system increase?

02:01:35.840 --> 02:01:43.180
Now, the way you can think about this performance, let's say that I have a scientific application where I process one terabyte of data.

02:01:43.659 --> 02:01:48.940
We'll start small scale, although modern applications, they usually process terabytes of data.

02:01:50.239 --> 02:01:52.880
So let's say I have a terabyte of data that I need to process.

02:01:53.260 --> 02:02:01.819
And I have on a computer, I can process that data at one gigabyte per second due to the nature of the computation that I'm doing with that computer.

02:02:02.460 --> 02:02:05.859
So this means that if I run the application on one single computer,

02:02:06.199 --> 02:02:09.679
it will take me 1,000 seconds to process the data.

02:02:10.179 --> 02:02:15.960
One terabyte is 1,000 gigabytes, one gigabyte per second means that it will take 1,000 seconds.

02:02:16.119 --> 02:02:16.519
Give or take.

02:02:17.739 --> 02:02:23.840
So if I can add another computer to the mix and if I can split my data,

02:02:24.099 --> 02:02:26.159
let's say equally between the two computers,

02:02:26.359 --> 02:02:29.399
this should allow me to then process the data faster.

02:02:29.399 --> 02:02:34.819
So if I use both computers at the same time, each computer will process 500 gigabytes of data.

02:02:35.519 --> 02:02:37.639
That's still at one gigabyte per second.

02:02:37.899 --> 02:02:42.579
It means that now I can process a lot of data in 500 seconds, again, give or take.

02:02:43.379 --> 02:02:45.779
And I can keep on adding computers.

02:02:46.139 --> 02:02:53.519
I can keep on adding cores, maybe on my processors, or increase the speed of the processors to try to reduce this execution time.

02:02:53.520 --> 02:02:56.480
but one thing that we usually

02:02:56.480 --> 02:02:59.880
do not take into consideration by default

02:02:59.880 --> 02:03:02.840
when we think about splitting the application in this way

02:03:02.840 --> 02:03:05.860
is that we assume that there's not going to be any communication

02:03:05.860 --> 02:03:08.620
between the computers and that's still the difference

02:03:08.620 --> 02:03:12.140
between a modern day mainframe and then a cluster of computers

02:03:12.140 --> 02:03:14.940
right so in a mainframe the communication

02:03:14.940 --> 02:03:16.580
between the different cores right

02:03:16.580 --> 02:03:21.280
can happen at very fast speeds or very low latencies

02:03:22.059 --> 02:03:26.679
But between computers that are now connected over cables in different racks, for example,

02:03:26.819 --> 02:03:28.800
it takes a little bit longer to transfer the data.

02:03:29.239 --> 02:03:35.159
We're limited by the laws of physics, in addition to the overhead of sending the data from one computer to the other.

02:03:35.960 --> 02:03:40.779
So, a lot of the times when we want to split an application across multiple computers,

02:03:41.539 --> 02:03:46.159
we forget to take into account that there's also an additional overhead that gets added,

02:03:46.279 --> 02:03:49.179
which is the overhead of transferring data between the computers.

02:03:49.180 --> 02:03:52.860
and we want to transfer data for various reasons.

02:03:53.240 --> 02:03:56.560
The main reason is if the algorithm requires us

02:03:56.560 --> 02:03:59.000
to synchronize the state between the machines.

02:03:59.800 --> 02:04:04.079
So sometimes an application can be penalized in a way

02:04:04.079 --> 02:04:06.940
where minimal communication,

02:04:07.340 --> 02:04:09.920
and those are called embarrassingly parallel applications,

02:04:09.920 --> 02:04:11.320
those scale very well.

02:04:11.740 --> 02:04:13.680
But there's a class of applications

02:04:13.680 --> 02:04:15.880
that actually require a lot of communication.

02:04:16.060 --> 02:04:17.740
For example, artificial intelligence

02:04:17.739 --> 02:04:19.819
requires a lot of communication.

02:04:20.300 --> 02:04:23.599
Even if you were to train a larger model across multiple computers,

02:04:23.880 --> 02:04:27.399
you need to synchronize that model at various stages of training

02:04:27.399 --> 02:04:30.639
so then there's a lot of communication that happens between the computers.

02:04:32.319 --> 02:04:35.399
And if you remember, from the memory hierarchy pyramid,

02:04:36.439 --> 02:04:39.479
the network speed, or network devices,

02:04:39.599 --> 02:04:41.539
actually sat at the bottom of the hierarchy.

02:04:41.979 --> 02:04:44.760
So they were the slowest in terms of performance.

02:04:45.500 --> 02:04:48.340
So even though you add more machines,

02:04:48.940 --> 02:04:51.640
your performance will increase up until a point

02:04:51.640 --> 02:04:55.720
where you have so many machines that you have to coordinate between them so much

02:04:55.720 --> 02:04:58.699
that you're spending most of the time communicating over the network

02:04:58.699 --> 02:04:59.800
rather than being processed.

02:05:00.340 --> 02:05:01.880
So the performance can decrease.

02:05:01.880 --> 02:05:06.619
So when people talk about increasing the scalability of a distributed system,

02:05:06.760 --> 02:05:10.980
it refers to how do we analyze how many compute nodes we can add

02:05:10.980 --> 02:05:12.460
before performance goes down

02:05:12.460 --> 02:05:19.319
and what kind of modifications do we have to do inside the algorithms to make them work well with multiple machines.

02:05:21.159 --> 02:05:26.899
So when it comes to the different areas or types of distributed systems,

02:05:27.060 --> 02:05:29.480
we'll talk about supercomputers as well very soon.

02:05:30.279 --> 02:05:36.420
I would say 99% of supercomputers nowadays are actually built as clusters of computers.

02:05:37.039 --> 02:05:38.460
So there's no one single mainframe.

02:05:39.199 --> 02:05:40.939
There's no one single supercomputer.

02:05:40.939 --> 02:05:51.419
99% of supercomputers in this world are built by connecting to very high-speed interconnects, brick-level algorithms, tens of thousands of server machines.

02:05:51.419 --> 02:06:01.159
So those servers are connected to high-speed. They have usually special cooling and special operating systems that they use to run and special programming languages and compilers.

02:06:01.779 --> 02:06:04.179
But this is how modern-day supercomputers are built.

02:06:04.180 --> 02:06:11.579
So, in order to run a very large application, people thought about how do we add multiple computers to increase the scalability of the system.

02:06:13.060 --> 02:06:16.680
And I know I mentioned, so we want to increase the scalability out of two reasons.

02:06:17.079 --> 02:06:21.600
So, in the world of supercomputers, we typically have one application that we want to run very fast.

02:06:21.600 --> 02:06:26.880
while in the world of let's say enterprise systems

02:06:26.880 --> 02:06:29.840
corporations and banks and stuff

02:06:29.840 --> 02:06:35.180
what we care is how many services we can provide per second

02:06:35.180 --> 02:06:37.260
how many requests we can provide a second

02:06:37.260 --> 02:06:41.960
think about a bank that wants to allow people to pay everything using their credit cards

02:06:41.960 --> 02:06:44.620
so the credit cards every single time you use it

02:06:44.620 --> 02:06:46.400
they have to send a request to the bank

02:06:46.400 --> 02:06:50.520
so the question is how many requests per second can the bank process

02:06:50.520 --> 02:06:53.840
to be able to satisfy as many clients as possible.

02:06:54.940 --> 02:06:58.080
So again, this is that area where we want to provide a service

02:06:58.080 --> 02:06:59.640
to an increasing number of users.

02:07:02.440 --> 02:07:06.900
However, when we talk about scalability,

02:07:07.580 --> 02:07:15.300
a lot of the times people focus on what we call size scalability.

02:07:15.400 --> 02:07:19.660
So this refers again to how do we increase the number of processing resources

02:07:19.659 --> 02:07:23.800
or the number of users in the system, or how do we make an application run faster.

02:07:23.800 --> 02:07:29.559
However, there are also two other different kinds of scalability that are also very important

02:07:29.559 --> 02:07:32.739
and sometimes we don't take it into account.

02:07:33.220 --> 02:07:37.359
So one of them is geographical scalability, which refers to what happens with the system

02:07:37.359 --> 02:07:41.539
when we increase the distance between the components of the system.

02:07:41.539 --> 02:07:46.319
and then administrative scalability,

02:07:46.640 --> 02:07:50.220
which refers to what happens when our distributed system

02:07:50.220 --> 02:07:53.880
spans across different administrative domains.

02:07:53.960 --> 02:07:55.579
We'll explain what these mean.

02:07:56.500 --> 02:08:02.659
For size scalability, usually the reason why we want to add more computers

02:08:02.659 --> 02:08:05.479
or more processing power or more resources

02:08:05.479 --> 02:08:09.140
is because we reach some sort of limitation in our system.

02:08:09.140 --> 02:08:12.820
So, this goes back to what we talked a little bit earlier about what a computer is.

02:08:13.000 --> 02:08:17.520
So, some limitations might have to do with limitations by the CPU.

02:08:17.900 --> 02:08:23.079
A processor can execute a certain number of instructions per second.

02:08:23.200 --> 02:08:28.240
Let's say you can overclock the processor, but it has a hard limit of how many calculations it can do.

02:08:28.640 --> 02:08:33.020
So, in order to be able to do more calculations, to process a larger application,

02:08:33.200 --> 02:08:37.700
or to provide services to multiple users, you just need more computational power.

02:08:38.119 --> 02:08:40.460
It gets more CPUs to do those computations.

02:08:41.300 --> 02:08:43.439
Other times, the limitations could be

02:08:43.439 --> 02:08:45.679
either memory or storage capacity.

02:08:45.880 --> 02:08:47.639
So your applications are large enough

02:08:47.639 --> 02:08:49.179
that you need a lot of RAM

02:08:49.179 --> 02:08:50.840
or you need a lot of storage to store them.

02:08:50.939 --> 02:08:52.319
This is very typical, for example,

02:08:52.399 --> 02:08:53.659
for database applications.

02:08:54.319 --> 02:08:56.659
So the Amazon store,

02:08:56.960 --> 02:08:58.539
they have a lot of products,

02:08:58.619 --> 02:08:59.340
a lot of users,

02:08:59.559 --> 02:09:03.139
so they need to store all of that information

02:09:03.139 --> 02:09:04.079
in an efficient way

02:09:04.079 --> 02:09:06.039
so that if somebody searches for something,

02:09:06.039 --> 02:09:08.399
and they can get the results of those products pretty fast.

02:09:09.319 --> 02:09:14.279
So one of their bottlenecks in their system

02:09:14.279 --> 02:09:16.180
is probably going to be storage and memory.

02:09:18.239 --> 02:09:20.880
Now, there are other distributed systems

02:09:20.880 --> 02:09:23.560
that rely more on sending data over the network.

02:09:23.800 --> 02:09:26.380
So maybe network bandwidth could become a limitation as well.

02:09:27.000 --> 02:09:29.239
So the solution would be to increase

02:09:29.239 --> 02:09:33.399
either the number of network cables that connect,

02:09:33.399 --> 02:09:34.640
the number of fiber optics,

02:09:34.640 --> 02:09:38.220
or to upgrade copper to fiber and so on and so forth.

02:09:40.000 --> 02:09:43.760
Now, when it comes to geographical scalability,

02:09:44.400 --> 02:09:49.400
this is where some assumptions that we hold

02:09:49.400 --> 02:09:52.119
when we build distributed systems,

02:09:52.880 --> 02:09:56.400
if they are, for example, in a local area network

02:09:56.400 --> 02:09:57.840
versus a wide area network,

02:09:58.200 --> 02:10:01.480
it might lead to different kinds of behavior inside the system.

02:10:01.479 --> 02:10:06.339
so typically when we think about a cluster of computers

02:10:06.339 --> 02:10:09.599
we think about a tightly coupled cluster of computers

02:10:09.599 --> 02:10:12.339
so it means that the machines they usually reside in the same room

02:10:12.339 --> 02:10:16.279
and they're connected to some sort of you know wired connection

02:10:16.279 --> 02:10:19.259
you know and there is some sort of symmetry there

02:10:19.259 --> 02:10:21.779
in how the connection is implemented right

02:10:21.779 --> 02:10:24.879
so there are some guarantees in when you send data over the network

02:10:24.879 --> 02:10:26.919
where you will receive it in a certain month of time

02:10:26.920 --> 02:10:40.940
But, for example, when you go to a wider network, let's say now that you have to distribute your system across the Internet, right, you have a few machines here, a few machines in New York, a few machines in Los Angeles, right?

02:10:40.980 --> 02:10:48.199
So once you distribute them this way, you're going to encounter, of course, different kinds of latency expectations.

02:10:48.440 --> 02:10:52.560
Latency means how fast you can send data from one computer to the other, right?

02:10:52.560 --> 02:11:03.060
Even if, for example, we were to implement a perfect software algorithm that sends the data at the speed of how fast the electron moves through the wires, right?

02:11:03.480 --> 02:11:06.820
The longer the wires, the more it takes time for the electron to actually move.

02:11:07.000 --> 02:11:09.720
So we're bounded at some point even by the laws of physics.

02:11:10.100 --> 02:11:16.520
Even for fiber optics, we're bounded by how fast the photon goes through the fiber optic to send data.

02:11:17.620 --> 02:11:19.220
So there are, of course, limitations.

02:11:19.220 --> 02:11:35.260
There are, we will see, types of applications, and the most popular types of applications are synchronous client-server applications, where basically you have a client application that requests a service from a server and just basically waits for the server to reply.

02:11:35.260 --> 02:11:39.220
The longer the distances, the slower the application will be.

02:11:39.980 --> 02:11:46.659
So these are things that we simply are very hard to, again, to overcome.

02:11:47.000 --> 02:11:52.500
And the solution would be to actually deploy more hardware closer to where the resources are needed.

02:11:52.820 --> 02:11:55.760
Just to give another example for geographical scalability,

02:11:56.500 --> 02:12:00.100
when Netflix started building their streaming services,

02:12:00.680 --> 02:12:04.980
they had to think about, okay, we can build, of course, the software to do the streaming and everything,

02:12:04.979 --> 02:12:08.619
but the question is, how do we locate data closer to where it's needed?

02:12:09.039 --> 02:12:10.039
So they had to think about,

02:12:10.139 --> 02:12:12.219
how do we build data centers across the world,

02:12:12.539 --> 02:12:14.539
initially across the country, then across the world,

02:12:14.919 --> 02:12:17.039
so that whenever there's a very popular movie

02:12:17.039 --> 02:12:18.979
that is in high demand in a certain region,

02:12:19.119 --> 02:12:21.459
we need that movie into those data centers

02:12:21.459 --> 02:12:23.519
so that it's closer to where it's needed.

02:12:23.579 --> 02:12:25.639
Because if we had only one data center

02:12:25.639 --> 02:12:29.539
and everyone wanted to watch their movie from that same data center,

02:12:29.659 --> 02:12:31.939
we're just limited by the network bandwidth.

02:12:31.979 --> 02:12:33.000
We just cannot.

02:12:33.000 --> 02:12:39.279
We have a certain level of, I don't know, terabytes per second that we can send, but

02:12:39.279 --> 02:12:40.340
we cannot go over that limit.

02:12:40.520 --> 02:12:46.659
So the only way would be to distribute or decentralize the system and move stuff around

02:12:46.659 --> 02:12:49.520
so that we can satisfy those requirements.

02:12:52.760 --> 02:12:57.239
When it comes to wide-area networks, so when we're connecting our distributed systems across

02:12:57.239 --> 02:13:02.840
the internet, sometimes the links or the connections between the machines becomes

02:13:02.840 --> 02:13:07.000
unreliable. So when you have a cluster of computers in the same room,

02:13:07.079 --> 02:13:11.079
you have copper cables, you have a certain guarantee that the only messages

02:13:11.079 --> 02:13:14.940
that are being sent are from the computers inside the room. But when you send data

02:13:14.940 --> 02:13:18.800
over the internet, we're not sending over one single copper wire.

02:13:19.159 --> 02:13:23.000
So, for example, when I'm contacting youtube.com from my phone,

02:13:23.079 --> 02:13:26.860
I'm sending data over the wireless network. If there are other

02:13:26.860 --> 02:13:30.699
computers that connect to the same wireless access point, there could be interference

02:13:30.699 --> 02:13:32.139
so my performance could be slow.

02:13:33.000 --> 02:13:34.179
But let's say that it's not, right?

02:13:34.220 --> 02:13:39.099
So it sends the data to a router, a wireless router.

02:13:39.239 --> 02:13:43.960
Then the router is probably connected to some sort of wire cable to another router, right?

02:13:44.139 --> 02:13:47.079
And then that router is connected to other routers and so on and so forth

02:13:47.079 --> 02:13:50.340
until it reaches, you know, maybe to fiber optics, it reaches my destination.

02:13:50.880 --> 02:13:55.099
But, for example, if I were to send data over the Internet,

02:13:55.800 --> 02:13:59.039
if, for example, the router that is supposed to forward my packet

02:13:59.039 --> 02:14:01.359
from one network to another dies, right?

02:14:01.399 --> 02:14:02.500
I have to resend the data.

02:14:02.800 --> 02:14:06.500
So that creates, again, a certain kind of uncertainty when it comes to sending data,

02:14:06.640 --> 02:14:09.239
which means that now when you program the applications,

02:14:09.699 --> 02:14:14.380
you have to program in a way where they can withstand that kind of data loss, let's say.

02:14:16.119 --> 02:14:19.319
That's why, for example, when it comes to streaming video,

02:14:19.460 --> 02:14:21.979
they have various kinds of solutions there.

02:14:22.899 --> 02:14:27.800
So when, for example, YouTube streams videos,

02:14:27.800 --> 02:14:32.600
they will send you the frames of the videos in a compressed form, and your program will decompress it.

02:14:32.900 --> 02:14:36.640
But if, for example, certain frames get lost, they don't get to be sent.

02:14:36.820 --> 02:14:43.460
Actually, they use the algorithms on the client side in your browser to actually try to interpolate the missing frames.

02:14:43.659 --> 02:14:45.739
So they try to predict what were the missing frames.

02:14:45.840 --> 02:14:48.220
That's why sometimes you might see like a blurry thing.

02:14:48.340 --> 02:14:50.039
Sometimes you see a little bit of lag there.

02:14:50.539 --> 02:14:52.100
So you see some inconsistencies.

02:14:52.180 --> 02:14:53.520
But those are bearable enough.

02:14:53.860 --> 02:14:59.420
People won't really be that bothered by them unless they're like fanatics about,

02:14:59.420 --> 02:15:01.720
oh, I want to have the most clear image always.

02:15:02.320 --> 02:15:03.900
But usually it's not the case.

02:15:04.700 --> 02:15:06.580
But anyways, but it depends on the application.

02:15:06.880 --> 02:15:13.140
For example, if you play a video game and you're playing a multiplayer game

02:15:13.140 --> 02:15:18.080
and you're part of an esports competition, then latency is very important.

02:15:18.260 --> 02:15:20.120
If you lose a frame, you're going to get a lag.

02:15:20.580 --> 02:15:23.140
Your character will be shot or destroyed or whatever.

02:15:23.140 --> 02:15:24.180
so you lose the game.

02:15:24.360 --> 02:15:27.039
So that's an example where things are very important.

02:15:27.119 --> 02:15:29.079
You don't want to lose data that well.

02:15:29.140 --> 02:15:31.000
That's why usually when they do esports tournaments,

02:15:31.119 --> 02:15:33.220
they do them in a confined area.

02:15:33.320 --> 02:15:34.560
They don't do them across the internet.

02:15:34.560 --> 02:15:36.100
They bring everyone to a location.

02:15:36.260 --> 02:15:38.180
They connect to dedicated cables,

02:15:38.320 --> 02:15:39.720
and they create a local area network

02:15:39.720 --> 02:15:41.380
that has more predictable performance.

02:15:42.520 --> 02:15:42.760
All right.

02:15:43.579 --> 02:15:46.500
And then the last point when it comes to geographical scalability

02:15:46.500 --> 02:15:48.579
is the lack of multipoint communication,

02:15:48.740 --> 02:15:50.980
and this has more to do with the way the protocols

02:15:50.980 --> 02:15:52.180
over the internet are implemented.

02:15:52.180 --> 02:15:53.640
We'll talk more about it.

02:15:54.119 --> 02:15:58.820
But basically, multi-point communication means multicast,

02:15:59.300 --> 02:16:02.340
where multicast means when you want, for example,

02:16:02.460 --> 02:16:05.260
to send a message to a subset of machines from your network.

02:16:05.600 --> 02:16:07.780
This can be implemented in local area networks,

02:16:07.880 --> 02:16:09.480
but it's typically very hard to implement

02:16:09.480 --> 02:16:10.920
over the wide area networks

02:16:10.920 --> 02:16:13.340
because you do not have control over the routers

02:16:13.340 --> 02:16:15.400
that are set up over the Internet.

02:16:16.000 --> 02:16:17.619
Actually, what I wanted to do is

02:16:17.619 --> 02:16:19.260
I wanted to also ask you a question

02:16:19.260 --> 02:16:20.579
because I'm talking about the Internet

02:16:20.579 --> 02:16:21.920
and I'm talking about networks.

02:16:22.180 --> 02:16:26.440
for again, students that have heard about it, you know, what is the difference between

02:16:26.440 --> 02:16:30.560
internet and intranet? Because I know it's kind of the difference between

02:16:30.560 --> 02:16:35.780
local internet network and wider network. But in terms of the definitions of these

02:16:35.780 --> 02:16:39.079
terms, you know, what is the difference between internet and intranet?

02:16:39.079 --> 02:16:46.899
Internet is in between one single corporation and internet is all over the world.

02:16:46.899 --> 02:16:54.279
true true so that's one way what about so what are the components of the

02:16:54.279 --> 02:17:02.420
internet and the components of internet so those are the type of networks right

02:17:02.420 --> 02:17:06.520
but the components you know what's what's connected inside an intranet and

02:17:06.520 --> 02:17:14.559
what's connected the side and in time so intranet right you have a network of

02:17:14.559 --> 02:17:19.600
devices. It can be, you know, you can have sub-networks as well, right? But an

02:17:19.600 --> 02:17:25.159
internet is a network of networks, right? So the way the internet works is you have

02:17:25.159 --> 02:17:29.619
multiple internet service providers that connect their own networks between each

02:17:29.619 --> 02:17:33.639
other and then allow people to connect their computers or their internets to

02:17:33.639 --> 02:17:40.059
them and then that's how we build the internet. Okay, so inside an internet you

02:17:40.059 --> 02:17:44.439
can have this multi-point communication because you control the devices by the

02:17:44.440 --> 02:17:48.280
Once you go across the internet, you're going to use somebody else's network,

02:17:48.720 --> 02:17:49.960
so you don't have control over it,

02:17:49.980 --> 02:17:52.920
so it's very hard to then implement this multipoint communication

02:17:52.920 --> 02:17:56.020
without using maybe overlay software.

02:17:56.840 --> 02:17:59.500
Okay, so you don't have built-in solutions.

02:18:00.000 --> 02:18:03.800
And some other limitations when you go geographically distributed,

02:18:04.200 --> 02:18:08.100
it also makes it very hard to identify resources.

02:18:08.940 --> 02:18:12.420
This is called naming in distributed systems,

02:18:12.520 --> 02:18:14.020
so how do you name the resources,

02:18:14.020 --> 02:18:16.079
how do I identify them in an inefficient way?

02:18:16.600 --> 02:18:17.319
We'll see these.

02:18:17.560 --> 02:18:20.440
We'll talk about some strategies that can be used to identify.

02:18:22.260 --> 02:18:25.319
Okay, now for administrative scalability.

02:18:26.940 --> 02:18:32.020
So this has to do with when you need to now scale your distributed system

02:18:32.020 --> 02:18:35.899
and it spans various administrative domains.

02:18:36.380 --> 02:18:41.120
So this means it spans various organizations or institutions

02:18:41.120 --> 02:18:43.640
or countries that have various policies.

02:18:44.020 --> 02:18:54.140
So, to give an example, let's say that you're Mark Zuckerberg, right, you're creating Facebook, this social network, you're revolutionizing, you know, the way people talk over the internet.

02:18:55.280 --> 02:18:58.720
And, right, one of the challenges, how do you build this to be efficient?

02:18:58.880 --> 02:19:06.560
How do you build this to scale in terms of size, right, the number of computers, the connections, right, so that everyone can talk in real time with each other?

02:19:06.559 --> 02:19:08.420
or not in real time because

02:19:08.420 --> 02:19:10.459
it's asynchronous, you're sending a message

02:19:10.459 --> 02:19:12.719
and the person can receive

02:19:12.719 --> 02:19:14.299
it, there's a little bit of a delay

02:19:14.299 --> 02:19:15.199
not a problem

02:19:15.199 --> 02:19:18.680
but one of the challenges that they

02:19:18.680 --> 02:19:20.159
had to face that is not

02:19:20.159 --> 02:19:22.680
discussed, it was more of a

02:19:22.680 --> 02:19:24.579
policy slash technical

02:19:24.579 --> 02:19:26.579
challenge, so when they built

02:19:26.579 --> 02:19:28.579
this application, they knew

02:19:28.579 --> 02:19:30.500
that they had users, they had to log in and

02:19:30.500 --> 02:19:32.479
whatever, but what happens if for example you

02:19:32.479 --> 02:19:34.180
want to run Facebook in a country

02:19:34.180 --> 02:19:35.639
that says only

02:19:35.639 --> 02:19:39.719
people that are 18 or old can use Facebook.

02:19:39.719 --> 02:19:43.719
Otherwise, you should deny them access to the platform versus a country that doesn't have

02:19:43.719 --> 02:19:47.500
that limitation versus a country that says, okay, you're allowed to

02:19:47.500 --> 02:19:51.639
publish this kind of information, but you're not allowed to publish political

02:19:51.639 --> 02:19:55.799
information. So then how do you implement this platform

02:19:55.799 --> 02:19:59.840
to support these different kinds of policies? So this is what

02:19:59.840 --> 02:20:03.879
it means when we talk about administrative scalability. It means how do we build

02:20:03.879 --> 02:20:08.899
a distributed system that can be tuned, that can work in different ways depending on the

02:20:08.899 --> 02:20:14.379
different policies that it spans.

02:20:14.379 --> 02:20:24.439
So, this is a challenge not only for enterprise systems like Google, Gmail, Google Docs and

02:20:24.439 --> 02:20:30.420
their communication services and Facebook and Twitter and all of these, but this can

02:20:30.420 --> 02:20:34.579
also, for example, appear as challenges even in the world of supercomputers.

02:20:35.379 --> 02:20:44.280
So what happened is that 30 years ago, as the United States was funding a lot of the

02:20:44.280 --> 02:20:51.440
national labs in the U.S. to build supercomputers, usually the supercomputers are hosted by the

02:20:51.440 --> 02:20:52.040
national lab.

02:20:52.139 --> 02:20:57.239
And each national lab has their own policies, has their own users, and has their own ways

02:20:57.239 --> 02:20:59.260
of storing the data on the computers,

02:20:59.459 --> 02:21:01.479
processing the data on the computers,

02:21:01.619 --> 02:21:02.260
so on and so forth.

02:21:02.760 --> 02:21:03.940
So as they were building

02:21:03.940 --> 02:21:05.639
these independent supercomputers

02:21:05.639 --> 02:21:06.879
that were competing with each other,

02:21:07.000 --> 02:21:07.619
at some point,

02:21:07.719 --> 02:21:09.479
the United States came and said,

02:21:09.780 --> 02:21:11.219
hey, what if I want to run

02:21:11.219 --> 02:21:13.139
a very large application,

02:21:13.440 --> 02:21:15.659
let's say for the Department of Defense,

02:21:15.920 --> 02:21:16.639
and I want to use

02:21:16.639 --> 02:21:18.059
all of the supercomputers,

02:21:18.199 --> 02:21:18.879
not just one.

02:21:19.159 --> 02:21:21.360
This is what they wanted to do

02:21:21.360 --> 02:21:22.000
during COVID.

02:21:22.219 --> 02:21:23.799
I want to process

02:21:23.799 --> 02:21:27.459
the genome of the virus.

02:21:27.679 --> 02:21:30.059
So I want to be able to use all of the computers,

02:21:30.159 --> 02:21:31.119
all of the supercomputers.

02:21:31.259 --> 02:21:32.739
And they hit a hard limitation.

02:21:33.579 --> 02:21:35.439
They hit a hard limitation because, again,

02:21:35.639 --> 02:21:38.099
different supercomputers have different policies,

02:21:39.599 --> 02:21:41.639
different rules on how the data was accessed

02:21:41.639 --> 02:21:42.699
and who's allowed to access,

02:21:42.840 --> 02:21:44.299
what kind of computation you want to run.

02:21:44.599 --> 02:21:46.420
So they had to figure out solutions

02:21:46.420 --> 02:21:49.279
on how to run an application

02:21:49.279 --> 02:21:50.959
across multiple supercomputers.

02:21:50.959 --> 02:21:53.559
So this gave birth to the notion

02:21:53.559 --> 02:21:55.119
of computational grids.

02:21:56.359 --> 02:22:01.840
So the grid is the solution that they came up with,

02:22:01.840 --> 02:22:07.519
which is basically a set of applications

02:22:07.519 --> 02:22:11.119
that virtualize the notion of computation in data.

02:22:11.959 --> 02:22:14.399
And then by using this notion of virtualization,

02:22:14.659 --> 02:22:16.719
they can then apply, they can implement it

02:22:16.719 --> 02:22:19.079
on various computers with various kinds of policies.

02:22:19.680 --> 02:22:22.600
Anyway, a lot of details, not really that important.

02:22:22.600 --> 02:22:33.720
Now, what I want to say is that there also exists a class of distributed applications that are actually immune to the challenges of administrative scalability.

02:22:34.659 --> 02:22:42.460
And usually the applications that fall into the area of peer-to-peer, let's say, distributed systems or peer-to-peer networks,

02:22:43.100 --> 02:22:48.800
they don't have to implement various kinds of policies for different kinds of reasons,

02:22:48.799 --> 02:22:53.039
because what they do is they push the responsibility of implementing the policies

02:22:53.039 --> 02:22:56.000
to the actual peers or the clients themselves.

02:22:56.719 --> 02:23:03.379
So they don't have to be implemented as part of the, let's say, network or the distributed system itself.

02:23:03.479 --> 02:23:06.759
And the best example for this is, of course, the BitTorrent protocol.

02:23:07.459 --> 02:23:12.739
I'm not sure if I'm too old at this point, and I'm the only one who knows about BitTorrent,

02:23:12.739 --> 02:23:14.599
but I have a feeling that you've heard about it as well,

02:23:14.759 --> 02:23:18.619
which is this protocol that can be used to share data.

02:23:18.799 --> 02:23:20.019
That's what I'm going to call it.

02:23:20.059 --> 02:23:23.000
Now, of course, the data that you share depends on the user.

02:23:23.299 --> 02:23:28.019
So the policy of what kind of data you want to transfer falls on the user,

02:23:28.179 --> 02:23:29.299
not on the system itself.

02:23:29.879 --> 02:23:32.759
The system is just meant to share data in a very efficient way.

02:23:32.759 --> 02:23:37.359
And there are, of course, there's a reason why they built the BitTorrent

02:23:37.359 --> 02:23:39.259
and it was a problem that they were trying to solve.

02:23:39.359 --> 02:23:43.179
And we have a dedicated set of slides for looking at the BitTorrent protocol

02:23:43.179 --> 02:23:44.500
that we will look at later.

02:23:44.500 --> 02:23:58.260
But just as a little preview, they were trying to solve the problem of transferring data from a personal computer to another personal computer in a very efficient way.

02:23:58.940 --> 02:24:02.239
The internet is built in an asymmetric way.

02:24:03.000 --> 02:24:07.420
So us, the regular users, we are considered consumers.

02:24:08.479 --> 02:24:13.420
While the people with data centers and servers, they are considered producers.

02:24:13.420 --> 02:24:21.860
So when you buy Internet from an Internet Service Provider, they will give you a download speed and then an upload speed.

02:24:21.960 --> 02:24:25.360
Usually, the upload speed is less than a download speed.

02:24:25.719 --> 02:24:34.120
So you can download at 100 megabits per second, 200 megabits per second, but usually you can only upload for like 10, 20 megabits per second.

02:24:34.120 --> 02:24:39.520
And it's because, again, when they design the Internet the first time, they design it in this producer-consumer way.

02:24:39.520 --> 02:24:42.360
where the servers, they can push more data

02:24:42.360 --> 02:24:44.880
and as the consumers, we can download more data.

02:24:44.960 --> 02:24:46.659
But now, what if you want to transfer data

02:24:46.659 --> 02:24:47.700
between your friends?

02:24:48.120 --> 02:24:50.140
You're limited by that upload speed.

02:24:50.280 --> 02:24:52.360
So this is where BitTorrent came in

02:24:52.360 --> 02:24:54.020
and they designed this clever protocol

02:24:54.020 --> 02:24:58.659
where they can create multiple replicas of your data

02:24:58.659 --> 02:25:01.760
and it allows you to download different chunks of your data

02:25:01.760 --> 02:25:03.880
from different sources simultaneously.

02:25:04.520 --> 02:25:07.420
Each source gets uploaded from the computer

02:25:07.420 --> 02:25:10.280
at a small speed, but you can download at a fast speed.

02:25:10.540 --> 02:25:12.719
So that was the main reason behind it.

02:25:12.719 --> 02:25:16.600
Now, the fact that we used it to transfer various other kinds of data,

02:25:16.760 --> 02:25:17.540
that's a different story.

02:25:17.899 --> 02:25:21.479
But again, the BitTorrent system itself is not responsible

02:25:21.479 --> 02:25:23.760
with implementing any kind of policy.

02:25:23.899 --> 02:25:26.040
That's all shifted towards the users.

02:25:26.540 --> 02:25:29.059
That's why the creators of the BitTorrent protocol,

02:25:29.200 --> 02:25:32.440
they cannot be held liable if people use it to transfer data illegally.

02:25:32.780 --> 02:25:35.219
It's actually the people themselves that are actually transferring

02:25:35.219 --> 02:25:36.500
the data online.

02:25:37.420 --> 02:25:40.239
So this applies also to other systems as well.

02:25:40.639 --> 02:25:44.280
The earlier versions of Skype were also implemented as a peer-to-peer system,

02:25:44.280 --> 02:25:47.440
but then once they got bought by Microsoft, it became a relay base,

02:25:47.680 --> 02:25:51.819
so they lost that, let's say, capability.

02:25:52.420 --> 02:25:55.280
And then Spotify as well can be peer-assisted.

02:25:56.020 --> 02:25:58.100
So for example, if you have multiple devices,

02:25:58.260 --> 02:26:01.540
they can assist each other actually by transferring if they have the same melody.

02:26:02.319 --> 02:26:05.020
Windows also has this as well when it does updates, right,

02:26:05.020 --> 02:26:08.380
He can detect if there's another computer in the same network that has the same updates,

02:26:08.640 --> 02:26:10.380
he can transfer the updates from that computer.

02:26:15.300 --> 02:26:15.780
Okay.

02:26:16.739 --> 02:26:23.600
Now, when it comes to scalability, usually people focus on size scalability the most.

02:26:23.600 --> 02:26:26.120
This is the main challenge that they're trying to solve.

02:26:26.640 --> 02:26:32.659
Geographic scalability and administrative scalability becomes relevant once you reach very high scales.

02:26:32.659 --> 02:26:36.479
So typically, company level scale, like large companies.

02:26:37.219 --> 02:26:39.920
So for us, what's more important is to understand, you know,

02:26:39.920 --> 02:26:43.860
how do we scale and distribute systems in terms of the size of the system?

02:26:44.000 --> 02:26:49.700
So we can, let's say, process an application faster or provide a service to multiple users.

02:26:50.319 --> 02:26:56.340
So there are three kinds of techniques that can be used to actually increase the size scalability of the system.

02:26:56.659 --> 02:26:59.619
One of them is to hide communication latencies.

02:26:59.620 --> 02:27:03.380
And this is something that we will see a little bit later when we will talk about communication.

02:27:04.360 --> 02:27:09.020
So, for example, applications could use asynchronous communication.

02:27:09.420 --> 02:27:14.360
So, asynchronous communication means that if I need some sort of service,

02:27:14.500 --> 02:27:19.079
I will send a request to the server and then I will wait until the server sends me back the reply.

02:27:19.840 --> 02:27:23.460
Asynchronous means that I can send a request and then do some other useful computation

02:27:23.460 --> 02:27:26.440
while I wait for the server to send me the reply.

02:27:26.440 --> 02:27:31.440
We will see, for example, web browsers actually implement asynchronous communication.

02:27:31.440 --> 02:27:36.440
It can, of course, have separate handlers for incoming responses.

02:27:36.440 --> 02:27:40.440
We will see that this can be implemented by using multiple threads and multiple cores

02:27:40.440 --> 02:27:42.440
that are available in your systems.

02:27:42.440 --> 02:27:46.440
The only issue is that, when it comes to hiding communication latency,

02:27:46.440 --> 02:27:49.440
is that not every application fits the model.

02:27:49.440 --> 02:27:52.440
Sometimes, the application might not have other computation to do,

02:27:52.440 --> 02:27:55.440
and it just has to wait for the data to come from this way.

02:27:55.440 --> 02:28:00.480
But this is one way that you can use to improve, let's say, the performance of applications.

02:28:01.980 --> 02:28:06.300
Another technique is you can move computation to the client, for example.

02:28:06.780 --> 02:28:10.200
This is a very popular technique that is used in web applications.

02:28:10.899 --> 02:28:17.220
So the first diagram here on the top shows the way web applications were built 20 years ago.

02:28:17.920 --> 02:28:23.319
So let's say, for example, the web application had a form where the user had to punch in some data, right,

02:28:23.319 --> 02:28:25.100
and the data had to be validated.

02:28:25.100 --> 02:28:28.579
Usually, the validation would happen on the server side, right?

02:28:28.659 --> 02:28:32.540
So, the user would type in the information, the information would be sent from the client

02:28:32.540 --> 02:28:36.760
to the server, the server will validate if it's incorrect, you let the client know, they

02:28:36.760 --> 02:28:42.159
type in the data again, and then this back and forth messaging happens between them until

02:28:42.159 --> 02:28:42.899
the form is correct.

02:28:43.600 --> 02:28:47.020
But, this is inefficient because you're sending a lot of data back and forth, right?

02:28:47.040 --> 02:28:51.380
What you want, you would like to be able to send a request in one go that is validated

02:28:51.380 --> 02:28:51.899
and correct.

02:28:51.899 --> 02:28:55.779
So, this is only validating the structure of the form, not the data itself.

02:28:56.039 --> 02:28:58.539
For that one, you still need the server's input.

02:28:59.299 --> 02:29:04.319
So, in the second diagram, this is what modern-day applications do, is they actually ship,

02:29:04.659 --> 02:29:08.840
they send part of the code from the server to your browser, to the client, right?

02:29:08.859 --> 02:29:13.479
And they can do that validation directly on your machine before they send the message to the server.

02:29:13.760 --> 02:29:17.440
That way, the bandwidth, right, is, you know, more clear, right?

02:29:17.440 --> 02:29:21.579
You don't have that much data being transferred, which means you can now add more clients simultaneously

02:29:21.579 --> 02:29:23.579
to send and receive these requests.

02:29:24.200 --> 02:29:26.200
So this is another technique that can be used

02:29:26.200 --> 02:29:30.680
to increase the scale of distributed systems.

02:29:32.100 --> 02:29:35.059
And then the most popular way to increase,

02:29:35.239 --> 02:29:38.319
let's say, the size, scalability of your system

02:29:38.319 --> 02:29:40.780
is to partition data and computation

02:29:40.780 --> 02:29:42.159
across multiple machines.

02:29:44.360 --> 02:29:48.139
So you can move both data and computation

02:29:48.139 --> 02:29:50.379
or separately depending on the situation.

02:29:50.379 --> 02:29:57.779
We will see in programming assignment 3, you will actually need to accelerate parts of your application.

02:29:58.619 --> 02:30:07.279
And the way you do that is by actually partitioning your data and the computation that happens with the data across multiple cores on your system.

02:30:07.280 --> 02:30:20.440
so you can move computation for example from servers to clients or from various peers

02:30:20.440 --> 02:30:27.040
you can use decentralized maintenance services so you partition the data across multiple computers

02:30:28.060 --> 02:30:34.320
you can decentralize your information system so that there's no one centralized entity that

02:30:34.319 --> 02:30:35.340
I could track everything.

02:30:35.819 --> 02:30:37.899
I can just go from one server to the other.

02:30:38.520 --> 02:30:44.399
For example, the internet was built by following the hyperning data structure

02:30:44.399 --> 02:30:47.860
or the hyperning structure for organizing information.

02:30:48.940 --> 02:30:52.500
So when we talk about how do we organize information on the computer,

02:30:53.039 --> 02:30:55.379
the most common way to organize data in a computer,

02:30:56.059 --> 02:31:00.139
mostly related to secondary storage, is the file system hierarchy.

02:31:00.760 --> 02:31:03.520
So you organize the data in the form of files and folders.

02:31:03.520 --> 02:31:08.760
right then you can have folders inside folders and files inside folders and

02:31:08.760 --> 02:31:12.399
this is one way to organize the data however over the internet we organize

02:31:12.399 --> 02:31:16.920
the data using the hyperlink structure so this means that we have web pages and

02:31:16.920 --> 02:31:21.460
web pages can point to other web pages so I can have a link to another web page

02:31:21.460 --> 02:31:25.620
so this is a decentralized way of organizing the data because you're

02:31:25.620 --> 02:31:29.800
going to have to have a root of your hierarchy right each web page can reside

02:31:29.799 --> 02:31:34.559
independently and can connect to other web pages independent.

02:31:34.559 --> 02:31:43.840
Alright another technique that is also popular when it comes to increasing the scale of

02:31:43.840 --> 02:31:47.639
distributed systems is replication and caching.

02:31:47.639 --> 02:31:54.319
So you make copies of the data available at different machines or level of the machines.

02:31:54.319 --> 02:31:57.399
So there is a difference actually between replication and caching.

02:31:57.399 --> 02:32:06.299
I know we sometimes maybe use it interchangeably, right, in day-to-day language, right, in day-to-day communication, but they actually mean different things.

02:32:06.420 --> 02:32:11.100
So usually replication happens on the server side while caching happens on the clients.

02:32:13.219 --> 02:32:18.559
So, again, when it comes to web applications, replication is heavily used.

02:32:18.559 --> 02:32:28.180
So actually the internet, whenever we are accessing a website for example, there is a very high chance that we are actually accessing a replica of the website and not the actual server.

02:32:28.739 --> 02:32:38.180
So the internet servers providers implement content distribution networks, which is a specialized distributed system that actually brings data closer to where the people are to where it's needed.

02:32:38.319 --> 02:32:38.559
Yes?

02:32:39.299 --> 02:32:40.459
Can I add a word over there?

02:32:40.719 --> 02:32:43.519
What is the difference between like cache and cookies?

02:32:45.199 --> 02:32:46.519
Cache and cookies.

02:32:48.559 --> 02:32:55.559
So cookies, they only store a specific kind of data while the cache...

02:32:55.559 --> 02:32:58.519
That's a good question.

02:32:58.519 --> 02:33:03.399
I think the difference is, again, more in terms of the purpose of it.

02:33:03.399 --> 02:33:09.879
So you're using the cookies to store some sort of session information so that you don't have to re-log in

02:33:09.879 --> 02:33:12.199
or re-authenticate every single time.

02:33:12.199 --> 02:33:16.180
While with caching, you're storing data that you're accessing often.

02:33:16.180 --> 02:33:22.520
So they're both kind of like a caching mechanism, but one is used for authentication while the

02:33:22.520 --> 02:33:26.800
other one is used for content.

02:33:26.800 --> 02:33:35.380
Alright so again, file servers and databases can be replicated, websites can be mirrored,

02:33:35.380 --> 02:33:40.920
of course modern day web browsers, they store caches of course including on your phone as

02:33:40.920 --> 02:33:44.620
well and that's why when you're accessing websites that don't change too often

02:33:44.620 --> 02:33:49.700
they load very fast because the webpage is already stored on your web browser.

02:33:49.700 --> 02:33:53.879
And then of course when you're transferring large amounts of data in the

02:33:53.879 --> 02:33:57.600
form of files, file caching is also another very popular solution but we'll

02:33:57.600 --> 02:34:01.280
see more, we'll talk in more detail about these techniques later. I just wanted to

02:34:01.280 --> 02:34:10.639
highlight them here. And of course so certain techniques that can be used to

02:34:10.639 --> 02:34:15.819
increase the size scalability even though it allows you to have multiple computers there

02:34:15.819 --> 02:34:20.859
maybe achieve redundancy they can also harm performance sometimes so for example with

02:34:20.859 --> 02:34:28.319
replication the main issue is that if you need to keep all of the replicas always synchronized

02:34:28.319 --> 02:34:33.799
right you can add extra overhead inside your system so as an example let's say that again

02:34:33.799 --> 02:34:40.679
I'm Google and I have Google Drive and I allow multiple people to access a Google Doc at

02:34:40.679 --> 02:34:41.819
the same time and modify.

02:34:42.459 --> 02:34:46.219
But let's say that the people, they actually reside at very large distances, right?

02:34:46.340 --> 02:34:50.859
So, and I have, right, the document, I want it to be replicated so that it's closer to

02:34:50.859 --> 02:34:51.379
the users.

02:34:51.639 --> 02:34:54.659
But now the problem is I have to keep all of those documents synchronized.

02:34:55.279 --> 02:34:55.599
So, right.

02:34:55.679 --> 02:35:00.139
So when I send a request to modify something in a document, I got to make sure that I modify

02:35:00.139 --> 02:35:05.420
all of the replicas before the other users see it so that everything is consistent.

02:35:05.699 --> 02:35:08.699
So this introduces extra messages that you have to send.

02:35:08.799 --> 02:35:12.760
So now instead of just sending one message to one server, you're sending multiple messages

02:35:12.760 --> 02:35:13.739
to multiple servers.

02:35:15.079 --> 02:35:20.319
So keeping the data consistent, you know, usually adds some sort of overhead, which

02:35:20.319 --> 02:35:24.000
actually sometimes harms performance and scalability.

02:35:24.000 --> 02:35:29.379
Now if there are applications that can tolerate inconsistencies

02:35:29.379 --> 02:35:31.059
then the problem can be avoided

02:35:31.059 --> 02:35:33.440
you don't get drawbacks

02:35:33.440 --> 02:35:35.319
but it depends on the application

02:35:39.319 --> 02:35:39.719
Okay

02:35:39.719 --> 02:35:40.600
Alright, so then

02:35:40.600 --> 02:35:43.340
the last thing that I want to do as part of the introduction

02:35:43.340 --> 02:35:45.559
is I want to talk about

02:35:45.559 --> 02:35:47.840
a classification of distributed systems

02:35:47.840 --> 02:35:49.360
so we finished talking about

02:35:49.360 --> 02:35:50.879
the design goals

02:35:50.879 --> 02:35:52.760
again, keep in mind design goals

02:35:52.760 --> 02:35:59.659
sharing of resources, distribution transparency, openness, dependability, security, and scalability.

02:35:59.860 --> 02:36:04.260
These are the main general goals that people follow when they build distributed systems.

02:36:04.800 --> 02:36:07.680
And then there are, of course, other sub-goals and objectives that we follow.

02:36:08.719 --> 02:36:14.579
So now what I want to do is I want to talk a little bit about an attempt to classify distributed systems

02:36:14.579 --> 02:36:17.659
into different kinds of distributed systems.

02:36:17.659 --> 02:36:23.239
And then after we're done with that, we'll talk about some of the pitfalls of distributive systems.

02:36:23.619 --> 02:36:26.739
And then we'll be able to move to architectures, finally.

02:36:27.280 --> 02:36:30.000
So I'm just going to brush over this pretty fast.

02:36:30.100 --> 02:36:31.840
You'll be able to find more details in the book.

02:36:32.680 --> 02:36:40.139
It's very hard to classify distributive systems just because there are many properties that different kinds of distributive systems have in common.

02:36:40.139 --> 02:36:49.000
We'll see at the end, when we look at hybrid, you know, double calls and distributed systems is basically a combination of various kinds of distributed systems.

02:36:49.180 --> 02:37:01.659
But the most important part, and again, from a historical point of view, distributed systems, let's say, became a thing because of the attempt to build faster computers.

02:37:02.539 --> 02:37:05.479
And the techniques that they used to build faster computers,

02:37:06.360 --> 02:37:12.180
the early techniques actually were looking at how do we parallelize various aspects of the computer.

02:37:13.079 --> 02:37:16.600
The earliest form of parallelization refers to instruction parallelism.

02:37:17.319 --> 02:37:22.420
So, you know, if we have a program, right, and parts of the program can be executed,

02:37:22.420 --> 02:37:25.180
let's say, you know, independently from other programs,

02:37:25.420 --> 02:37:27.879
the questions can be executed as part simultaneously.

02:37:28.579 --> 02:37:31.280
So that's one form of instruction level parallelism.

02:37:31.280 --> 02:37:45.239
There's also, for example, if we're processing a vector of data and we're doing an addition element by element, why can we design computer systems that can actually do an addition on four elements at a time to increase the performance of the application?

02:37:46.460 --> 02:37:50.140
So this led to various kinds of designs of processors.

02:37:51.220 --> 02:38:00.340
And this led actually to an attempt to create a supercomputer that actually used multiple processors.

02:38:00.340 --> 02:38:13.280
So it is very common nowadays when you go to a data center and when you look at a server, the server will have a motherboard and typically have not just one processor, but you will have maybe two or four processors connected on one motherboard.

02:38:13.479 --> 02:38:19.159
So that's one way to increase performance by adding more processors so you can compute more things in Petro.

02:38:19.159 --> 02:38:25.979
This technique evolved into the notion of multi-core

02:38:25.979 --> 02:38:29.559
but we'll spend more time in the processes chapter

02:38:29.559 --> 02:38:31.579
to understand what a core is

02:38:31.579 --> 02:38:33.719
and what's the difference between a core and a processor

02:38:33.719 --> 02:38:36.319
but for now just think about these multiple cores

02:38:36.319 --> 02:38:37.739
as being multiple processors

02:38:37.739 --> 02:38:42.139
Now what was very interesting about these multi-processor

02:38:42.139 --> 02:38:43.299
and multi-core system

02:38:43.299 --> 02:38:46.079
is that they were connected inside a computer

02:38:46.079 --> 02:38:53.239
in which all of the processors had access to the entire memory space of that computer.

02:38:53.539 --> 02:38:55.780
And this was achieved through some sort of interconnect.

02:38:56.219 --> 02:38:58.180
So you'd have the processors themselves,

02:38:58.620 --> 02:39:02.780
some sort of common interconnect that allowed all of the processors to access all of the memory.

02:39:04.239 --> 02:39:06.700
So this is called a shared memory system.

02:39:08.100 --> 02:39:11.200
So the shared memory system means that all of the cores of the processors

02:39:11.200 --> 02:39:12.440
can access the memory.

02:39:12.940 --> 02:39:15.180
Now, there are two kinds of shared memory systems.

02:39:15.180 --> 02:39:20.500
You have uniform memory access systems and non-uniform memory access systems.

02:39:20.720 --> 02:39:22.159
So UMA and NUMA.

02:39:23.159 --> 02:39:29.440
Uniform memory access systems are a type of systems in which all of the processors inside that system

02:39:29.440 --> 02:39:35.819
can access any byte of memory from any memory module at the exact same speed.

02:39:35.819 --> 02:39:38.899
non-uniform memory systems,

02:39:39.440 --> 02:39:43.299
they have the property that

02:39:43.299 --> 02:39:44.459
a processor

02:39:44.459 --> 02:39:48.479
will be able to access

02:39:48.479 --> 02:39:51.100
a certain memory region

02:39:51.100 --> 02:39:52.319
at a certain speed,

02:39:52.440 --> 02:39:54.199
sometimes faster if it's closer,

02:39:54.940 --> 02:39:56.819
and then will be able to access

02:39:56.819 --> 02:39:58.979
the memory of another memory region

02:39:58.979 --> 02:40:01.379
at a lower speed because it's farther away.

02:40:01.379 --> 02:40:03.680
Or because the interconnectors

02:40:03.680 --> 02:40:11.760
not implement an all-to-all communication between the processors and the memory so this is something

02:40:11.760 --> 02:40:18.639
that we don't really encounter that often when we buy regular computers however there was one chip

02:40:18.639 --> 02:40:25.360
that actually had non-uniform memory access so most processors that we buy nowadays they come

02:40:25.360 --> 02:40:31.000
configured with uniform memory access so all of the cores inside your processor will be able to

02:40:31.000 --> 02:40:36.860
access the memory of your system at the same speed. However, when AMD released the first and

02:40:36.860 --> 02:40:41.719
second generation Threadreadweaver processor, right, it was the first processor, they had a lot of cores,

02:40:41.719 --> 02:40:47.899
right, 32, 64 cores, right, it was very large. Now, the way that they built this processor is

02:40:47.899 --> 02:40:55.459
actually by combining together multiple smaller processors. So this meant that the first and the

02:40:55.459 --> 02:41:01.779
second generation processors, it means that they implemented non-uniform memory access. So the chip

02:41:01.779 --> 02:41:09.519
itself had, let's say, eight channels to connect to memory DIMMs, to memory chips. But the way it

02:41:09.519 --> 02:41:15.879
was organized is that you had four different, let's say, zones in your processor, and each zone

02:41:15.879 --> 02:41:21.019
connected directly to two of those channels. And then the connection to the rest of the channels

02:41:21.020 --> 02:41:23.079
was done through a lower speed interpreter.

02:41:23.920 --> 02:41:27.940
So this means now that even though you had 64 cores in one processor,

02:41:28.159 --> 02:41:33.380
it meant that 16 cores were able to access a quarter of your memory very fast,

02:41:33.819 --> 02:41:35.680
but the remaining three quarters very low.

02:41:35.960 --> 02:41:39.120
So now the challenge was when you wrote that program,

02:41:39.260 --> 02:41:42.920
you had to write it in a way where there was minimal communication

02:41:42.920 --> 02:41:46.220
between these different NUMA regions

02:41:46.220 --> 02:41:49.020
if you wanted to get the best performance.

02:41:49.020 --> 02:41:51.200
Otherwise, you would see performance degradation.

02:41:52.079 --> 02:41:57.860
Now, after they perfected their technology, starting with the third generation Threadripper,

02:41:58.200 --> 02:42:00.920
they were able to make it uniform memory access.

02:42:01.440 --> 02:42:05.640
However, in the BIOS, you can go and change it, and you can still enable, if you want,

02:42:06.020 --> 02:42:09.319
non-uniform memory access, which allow it to get even higher performance.

02:42:10.800 --> 02:42:10.979
Okay.

02:42:11.280 --> 02:42:14.560
So, anyway, these are, again, the shared memory systems.

02:42:14.800 --> 02:42:18.540
There are, of course, a few advantages when using shared memory systems, right?

02:42:18.540 --> 02:42:20.380
You can write one single program.

02:42:20.700 --> 02:42:22.500
They can run on all of these processors.

02:42:22.900 --> 02:42:27.520
We'll see very soon in the third chapter, we'll talk about threads.

02:42:28.080 --> 02:42:29.500
It's a mechanism that you can use.

02:42:30.160 --> 02:42:33.360
And again, if you want to access any region of memory,

02:42:33.560 --> 02:42:36.620
you just pass the address of that memory that you want to access.

02:42:38.060 --> 02:42:41.040
But again, these systems became very expensive to build.

02:42:41.420 --> 02:42:45.500
The most important component here in shared memory systems

02:42:45.500 --> 02:42:48.540
is actually the interconnect between the processors and the memory.

02:42:49.399 --> 02:42:52.680
You might have heard a few years ago, maybe two years ago,

02:42:53.479 --> 02:42:56.920
again, if you watch tech news and stuff,

02:42:57.000 --> 02:43:01.100
you might have heard some controversies regarding the Intel,

02:43:01.239 --> 02:43:06.920
I think 13 and maybe 14 generation processors that were starting to fail.

02:43:07.639 --> 02:43:12.360
So people were complaining that they were getting blue skin of death on the system.

02:43:12.459 --> 02:43:13.659
They were not working very well.

02:43:13.659 --> 02:43:19.079
and what was happening actually with those chips is that they were overclocked so the again the

02:43:19.079 --> 02:43:24.959
system was meant to was it was tuned to you know run very high speeds which generate a lot of heat

02:43:24.959 --> 02:43:30.819
and the heat ended up actually damaging the interconnect between the cores and the various

02:43:30.819 --> 02:43:36.959
other components which then damaged the chip and you're not able to use it. So again the interconnect

02:43:36.959 --> 02:43:44.359
is very important usually dictates the price of the processor so the higher

02:43:44.359 --> 02:43:49.799
speed the interconnect usually the more expensive the prices but people also

02:43:49.799 --> 02:43:53.219
looked at you know how do we build computers differently in a way where it's

02:43:53.219 --> 02:43:57.639
cheaper but maybe we can still get very good performance so they also started

02:43:57.639 --> 02:44:04.799
building private memory systems or those computer classes that I mentioned so in

02:44:04.799 --> 02:44:12.599
In a private memory system, we still have a bunch of processors, but each processor is connected to its own memory module,

02:44:12.920 --> 02:44:18.859
and then we usually either connect the processor or we connect the machine in which the processor is running

02:44:18.859 --> 02:44:22.659
to some sort of interconnect to other machines that have their own independent processors.

02:44:23.099 --> 02:44:28.719
So in a private memory system, the processor only has access to its own local memory,

02:44:28.859 --> 02:44:31.340
but it does not have access to the memory of the other processors.

02:44:32.200 --> 02:44:45.159
So if we want to transfer data, for example, between two processors, what we have to do is we have to copy that data to the interconnect, send it to the other processor, store it somewhere locally, and then we can process it.

02:44:45.840 --> 02:44:52.100
So modern-day supercomputers, they use a combination of private and shared memory architectures.

02:44:52.139 --> 02:45:00.920
So usually on a single node, on a single server, they use a shared memory architecture, but then across multiple computers, they use a private memory architecture.

02:45:01.340 --> 02:45:04.239
Okay.

02:45:06.579 --> 02:45:08.020
So, again, some observation.

02:45:08.600 --> 02:45:14.460
So, multiprocessor and multi-core applications, they're typically easier to program because you just have to write a program.

02:45:14.579 --> 02:45:20.079
You don't have to implement any kind of mechanisms in your program where you have to transfer data from one memory to another.

02:45:20.579 --> 02:45:24.399
You just access the various, you know, memory regions that you have on your computer.

02:45:24.399 --> 02:45:28.619
people have tried over the years

02:45:28.619 --> 02:45:34.779
to implement some sort of virtual shared memory

02:45:34.779 --> 02:45:37.539
for private memory systems

02:45:37.539 --> 02:45:40.379
however these are very difficult to implement

02:45:40.379 --> 02:45:43.059
and very difficult to implement in an efficient way

02:45:43.059 --> 02:45:46.059
because the virtual memory techniques

02:45:46.059 --> 02:45:49.440
have a very hard time predicting what the user wants to do

02:45:49.440 --> 02:45:51.440
or what are the next instructions

02:45:51.960 --> 02:45:57.140
Usually, the programmers of these supercomputers or distributive systems,

02:45:57.140 --> 02:45:58.800
they know the application very well.

02:45:59.180 --> 02:46:00.700
They know the data movement very well,

02:46:00.760 --> 02:46:05.560
and they can make certain decisions that a general virtual memory cannot.

02:46:06.500 --> 02:46:11.540
So that's why even though people have tried to implement them in practice,

02:46:11.860 --> 02:46:14.640
people, when they program these supercomputers,

02:46:14.700 --> 02:46:17.780
they use specialized programming languages in which they explicitly say

02:46:17.780 --> 02:46:20.620
which data has to be transferred at which moment of time.

02:46:20.620 --> 02:46:24.000
So having control over that allows you to actually get the best performance.

02:46:29.340 --> 02:46:35.360
So when it comes to cluster computing, most supercomputers out there, they usually follow this architecture.

02:46:37.400 --> 02:46:47.320
The cluster of computers, they're typically implemented as a group of high-end computing systems connected to some sort of high-speed interconnect.

02:46:48.100 --> 02:46:52.680
Some properties of computer clusters is that the cluster is homogeneous.

02:46:53.379 --> 02:46:57.680
So it means that usually the compute nodes that are part of the cluster, they are the same.

02:46:58.219 --> 02:47:00.500
This makes it different from an enterprise system.

02:47:00.639 --> 02:47:04.219
So in an enterprise system, you might have different servers with different architecture,

02:47:04.379 --> 02:47:08.619
different amount of RAM, connected with different kinds of speeds that get added together,

02:47:08.619 --> 02:47:11.379
that get expanded over time.

02:47:11.539 --> 02:47:13.020
So the data center gets expanded over time.

02:47:13.020 --> 02:47:19.700
But when people build a supercomputer, they typically build it with the same kind of compute nodes connected in a symmetric, let's say, network.

02:47:20.880 --> 02:47:24.360
So homogeneous also means that they're running the same operating system.

02:47:24.700 --> 02:47:26.800
And again, they have almost identical hardware.

02:47:28.479 --> 02:47:32.860
And then they might or might not have managing nodes.

02:47:32.979 --> 02:47:36.399
So these managing nodes are a little bit of interesting history.

02:47:36.840 --> 02:47:40.540
Modern-rate supercomputers, they do have managing nodes, but they play a different role.

02:47:41.420 --> 02:47:45.300
but I kind of want to say 20 years ago,

02:47:46.280 --> 02:47:48.560
there was a supercomputer built at Argon

02:47:48.560 --> 02:47:50.320
called the IBM BlueGene,

02:47:50.600 --> 02:47:51.680
which was very interesting.

02:47:51.920 --> 02:47:53.080
So the compute nodes

02:47:53.080 --> 02:47:55.560
actually did not have any logic

02:47:55.560 --> 02:47:58.100
that helped them to load an operating system.

02:47:58.660 --> 02:47:59.800
So the compute nodes

02:47:59.800 --> 02:48:01.660
were very simple computational,

02:48:01.980 --> 02:48:02.820
let's say, chips

02:48:02.820 --> 02:48:05.500
that you had to load the program into them

02:48:05.500 --> 02:48:06.360
and then you would already know

02:48:06.360 --> 02:48:07.620
how to execute one program.

02:48:07.760 --> 02:48:08.220
So technically,

02:48:08.360 --> 02:48:09.880
there was no operating system at all.

02:48:10.299 --> 02:48:12.259
So they only knew how to run one single program.

02:48:13.000 --> 02:48:21.539
So that supercomputer had a bunch of management nodes that ran typical, let's say, computer architectures, like the x86 architecture.

02:48:21.699 --> 02:48:28.519
And those management nodes would actually load the parallel supercomputer program on all of the chips in parallel.

02:48:29.039 --> 02:48:32.939
And then it would start all of them simultaneously to run the application.

02:48:33.179 --> 02:48:35.359
Once the application was done, the chip would shut down.

02:48:36.159 --> 02:48:39.179
So anyways, very extreme ways of running computers.

02:48:39.300 --> 02:48:39.920
We don't do that.

02:48:40.600 --> 02:48:44.300
You're not going to turn the computer only to run one program and then shut it off.

02:48:44.460 --> 02:48:46.800
You run multiple things, you know, they usually happen.

02:48:47.399 --> 02:48:50.200
But certain supercomputers were designed in this way.

02:48:51.100 --> 02:48:54.940
And then the last aspect is that usually in supercomputers,

02:48:55.159 --> 02:48:59.079
they tend to decouple the various components of a computer.

02:48:59.720 --> 02:49:04.200
So we talked at the beginning about the processor, memory, and the network and disk.

02:49:04.659 --> 02:49:07.600
In a supercomputer, they tend to separate these

02:49:07.600 --> 02:49:11.420
and put computation memory in one type of compute node,

02:49:11.780 --> 02:49:14.300
put storage in another kind of compute node,

02:49:14.560 --> 02:49:16.320
and then they use this high-speed interconnect

02:49:16.320 --> 02:49:19.180
to actually transfer data from the storage to the compute nodes

02:49:19.180 --> 02:49:20.480
whenever they have to do computation.

02:49:23.400 --> 02:49:24.079
All right.

02:49:25.700 --> 02:49:28.880
And then, of course, we talked about grid computing as well,

02:49:29.000 --> 02:49:33.340
which is, again, this attempt to connect multiple supercomputers

02:49:33.340 --> 02:49:34.579
from various organizations.

02:49:34.799 --> 02:49:36.260
We're not really going to spend a lot of time.

02:49:37.479 --> 02:49:37.639
Okay.

02:49:40.719 --> 02:49:41.200
All right.

02:49:41.600 --> 02:49:46.559
So, again, one class of distributed systems is this class of petro computers

02:49:46.559 --> 02:49:49.600
or classes of computers, supercomputers, you can call them.

02:49:49.739 --> 02:49:49.860
Okay.

02:49:50.360 --> 02:49:52.500
They were designed and they worked in a specific way.

02:49:52.500 --> 02:49:58.479
Now, distributed systems have also been adopted by the enterprise world.

02:49:58.620 --> 02:49:59.979
So this is by corporations.

02:50:00.579 --> 02:50:00.639
Right.

02:50:00.639 --> 02:50:06.119
So back in the day, they used to have a lot of humans that do accounting and keep track of various transactions.

02:50:06.559 --> 02:50:11.420
But once computers became accessible, they started adopting this technology to automate their process.

02:50:11.939 --> 02:50:16.139
So we can store spreadsheets nowadays, databases with information, transactions.

02:50:16.340 --> 02:50:17.920
They can all happen inside computers.

02:50:19.219 --> 02:50:24.299
And in the early days, when they built these computers, they were built in a way where they were not networked.

02:50:24.519 --> 02:50:25.939
So the computers were separated.

02:50:27.000 --> 02:50:28.459
There were different organizations.

02:50:28.459 --> 02:50:31.479
and at some point it became very beneficial

02:50:31.479 --> 02:50:33.619
to actually maybe connect these computers

02:50:33.619 --> 02:50:35.239
in a network and have them communicate

02:50:35.239 --> 02:50:35.919
with each other.

02:50:37.419 --> 02:50:39.379
So in the world of

02:50:39.379 --> 02:50:41.239
enterprise systems, this

02:50:41.239 --> 02:50:43.219
problem of how do you connect them

02:50:43.219 --> 02:50:45.199
and how do you make the applications work well

02:50:45.199 --> 02:50:47.519
is called a problem

02:50:47.519 --> 02:50:49.039
of integrating applications.

02:50:50.000 --> 02:50:51.379
Sometimes the applications were

02:50:51.379 --> 02:50:53.539
from the same organization, but sometimes the applications

02:50:53.539 --> 02:50:55.039
were from different organizations.

02:50:55.319 --> 02:50:57.739
Just to give an example, let's say

02:50:57.739 --> 02:51:03.600
that you have a company like United that manages flights,

02:51:04.020 --> 02:51:09.360
another company like Hilton Hotels that manages hotels bookings,

02:51:09.520 --> 02:51:13.459
and then another company, let's say Hertz, that manages car rentals.

02:51:14.180 --> 02:51:18.440
And all of a sudden comes, let's say, Expedia or Priceline

02:51:18.440 --> 02:51:20.879
that decides that, hey, I want to build a system

02:51:20.879 --> 02:51:25.539
that actually allows users to book a flight and a hotel and a car

02:51:25.539 --> 02:51:27.459
all in one single interface.

02:51:27.739 --> 02:51:30.579
from all of these different users.

02:51:30.920 --> 02:51:32.799
So now, all of a sudden, I have to figure out

02:51:32.799 --> 02:51:36.219
how do I combine the data from all of these databases

02:51:36.219 --> 02:51:39.840
and how do I create operations over this data

02:51:39.840 --> 02:51:42.020
that is consistent with all of these databases.

02:51:43.039 --> 02:51:44.860
So people have to think about,

02:51:44.860 --> 02:51:49.000
okay, how do we combine these enterprise applications?

02:51:49.340 --> 02:51:51.860
So this is the enterprise application integration,

02:51:52.239 --> 02:51:52.979
let's say, problem.

02:51:54.539 --> 02:51:57.180
Some of the earlier techniques that they used

02:51:57.180 --> 02:52:03.319
were tied to the fact that a lot of the applications in the enterprise world are designed around

02:52:03.319 --> 02:52:03.940
databases.

02:52:04.700 --> 02:52:10.280
So when you go to a corporation, they usually use databases to manage transactions, to manage

02:52:10.280 --> 02:52:12.479
operations, to manage accounts.

02:52:13.020 --> 02:52:14.860
So this is all done using databases.

02:52:14.860 --> 02:52:22.960
So this actually gave the people who were designing the systems the opportunity to actually

02:52:22.960 --> 02:52:25.239
using some existing solutions.

02:52:26.079 --> 02:52:31.780
So if, for example, I'm Priceline and I want to build this bigger application,

02:52:32.239 --> 02:52:36.440
what I can do is I can use the notion of nested transactions.

02:52:37.000 --> 02:52:40.899
So if somebody comes to Priceline and they want to book a hotel and an airplane,

02:52:41.579 --> 02:52:45.520
I can create a bigger transaction made out of smaller transactions,

02:52:45.959 --> 02:52:47.780
and then I can deploy these transactions independently.

02:52:48.539 --> 02:52:52.280
And only if these two transactions complete successfully,

02:52:52.379 --> 02:52:54.280
then the entire transaction completes successfully.

02:52:54.280 --> 02:52:55.600
Otherwise, I need to roll back.

02:52:56.280 --> 02:53:05.980
So to implement this, people created a type of distributed system called the Transaction Processing Monitor, the TP Monitor.

02:53:06.540 --> 02:53:13.159
So this was, again, a generalized solution that allowed people to connect multiple database applications

02:53:13.159 --> 02:53:19.800
and allowed client applications to send one big request, right, to break it down into small requests

02:53:19.800 --> 02:53:22.300
that can be processed independently by various servers.

02:53:22.299 --> 02:53:25.599
and only if all of the sub requests were completed successfully,

02:53:25.739 --> 02:53:28.359
the entire BN was completed successfully as well.

02:53:29.219 --> 02:53:29.459
Right?

02:53:29.479 --> 02:53:31.619
Because you don't want, when you go to Priceline,

02:53:31.619 --> 02:53:35.759
to get hotel, car, and airplane ticket, right?

02:53:35.819 --> 02:53:36.799
But then one of them fails.

02:53:37.659 --> 02:53:37.779
Right?

02:53:37.840 --> 02:53:41.199
You want all three of them to succeed in one transaction when you buy it.

02:53:42.340 --> 02:53:42.739
All right.

02:53:43.079 --> 02:53:46.139
So, again, this was one of the earlier solutions, let's say,

02:53:46.259 --> 02:53:48.840
to how do we integrate applications.

02:53:49.559 --> 02:53:52.159
But there also came the question, you know,

02:53:52.299 --> 02:53:55.239
because they were tied to the databases,

02:53:55.519 --> 02:53:57.759
what if two applications wanted to communicate

02:53:57.759 --> 02:53:58.939
between each other directly?

02:53:59.959 --> 02:54:00.059
Right?

02:54:00.219 --> 02:54:01.699
So maybe, you know,

02:54:01.799 --> 02:54:05.519
less expensive to create a database transaction

02:54:05.519 --> 02:54:07.319
just to implement a very simple communication

02:54:07.319 --> 02:54:08.159
between applications.

02:54:08.799 --> 02:54:10.199
So then they had to figure out

02:54:10.199 --> 02:54:11.259
what are the other ways

02:54:11.259 --> 02:54:12.779
that we can implement communication

02:54:12.779 --> 02:54:14.879
between various kinds of applications.

02:54:15.539 --> 02:54:17.599
So in the world of,

02:54:17.599 --> 02:54:20.219
let's say, enterprise systems,

02:54:20.219 --> 02:54:23.879
There were two kinds of communication technologies that were developed.

02:54:24.459 --> 02:54:30.159
One of them is called Remote Procedure Call, which we will study in depth in this class,

02:54:30.299 --> 02:54:34.639
and the other one is Message Oriented Middleware, which we'll also study in depth in this class.

02:54:35.559 --> 02:54:38.739
These are two ways that you can implement communication,

02:54:39.379 --> 02:54:42.639
and they can simplify the process of integrating applications.

02:54:43.420 --> 02:54:49.000
Both Remote Procedure Call and Message Oriented Middleware are implemented at the middleware level.

02:54:49.000 --> 02:54:57.500
If you remember when we talked in the lecture recording, we talked about distribution transparency and how it can be implemented using this middleware layer.

02:54:57.940 --> 02:55:01.459
Inside the middleware, we can also implement communication between applications.

02:55:02.040 --> 02:55:06.579
And we will see that there are various kinds of communication styles that can be implemented.

02:55:08.459 --> 02:55:08.700
Okay.

02:55:10.159 --> 02:55:16.579
Now, when it comes to integrating applications, if I were to ask you, hey, you have two programs running on the same computer, right?

02:55:16.579 --> 02:55:18.819
And I want you to implement communication between them.

02:55:18.819 --> 02:55:23.600
The easiest thing that you can do is you can create a common file between those two programs.

02:55:23.739 --> 02:55:27.299
One program can write to the file and the other program can read from that file.

02:55:27.600 --> 02:55:28.600
Boom, you have communication.

02:55:28.920 --> 02:55:30.699
It's not the most efficient, but it works.

02:55:30.779 --> 02:55:34.879
And it was one of the earlier techniques that people used in the enterprise world.

02:55:36.079 --> 02:55:38.819
Shared databases is another way to implement communication.

02:55:39.000 --> 02:55:42.520
And then more effective ways are, of course, remote procedure call and messaging.

02:55:43.159 --> 02:55:46.659
In remote procedure call, what the systems do

02:55:46.659 --> 02:55:51.720
is they allow you to actually call a function or a procedure on a different server.

02:55:52.440 --> 02:55:56.640
And it uses the exact same semantics that you would use when you write a program.

02:55:56.819 --> 02:55:59.640
So it makes it very easy for you to implement that communication.

02:55:59.940 --> 02:56:07.600
You will see in this course, as part of designing your, let's say, client-server application,

02:56:08.180 --> 02:56:11.200
I will ask you to use various kinds of communication technologies.

02:56:11.200 --> 02:56:17.200
We will start with a low level, which will be very hard because we have to do a lot of operations to make communication work.

02:56:17.579 --> 02:56:24.320
But then, we'll get a chance to implement the exact same application by using the remote procedure call, which will simplify things.

02:56:24.760 --> 02:56:31.560
We'll basically be able to write a program that basically calls functions from an object that resides on a different computer.

02:56:32.540 --> 02:56:39.980
And all of this, you know, communication is implemented by the remote procedure call middleware that you will need to start as part of that application.

02:56:39.979 --> 02:56:44.500
you will see it will simplify things and the messaging allows you to actually

02:56:44.500 --> 02:56:48.479
implement various kinds of messaging styles so synchronous asynchronous

02:56:48.479 --> 02:56:52.939
persistent transient we'll see what the differences between them are basically

02:56:52.939 --> 02:56:57.359
allows you to decouple time in space when we send messages around and these are

02:56:57.359 --> 02:57:01.399
also very popular ways of sending data not just only in enterprise systems

02:57:01.399 --> 02:57:05.020
nowadays but also inside cloud systems as well

02:57:05.020 --> 02:57:13.100
yes into another computer machine basically when we are sending one

02:57:13.100 --> 02:57:17.560
executing a command it is a remote procedure call, can it be called an

02:57:17.560 --> 02:57:24.020
example of RPC? No no so ssh does not use remote procedure call to implement

02:57:24.020 --> 02:57:33.960
that communication. In that ssh we are also executing... yeah you can run commands

02:57:33.959 --> 02:57:42.199
I know you can run commands and applications, but the way the underlying implementation of SSH is actually using messages.

02:57:42.939 --> 02:57:44.799
It does not use remote procedures.

02:57:45.519 --> 02:57:50.179
So when you use SSH, you don't call a function from another program.

02:57:50.799 --> 02:57:52.000
You can call programs.

02:57:52.079 --> 02:57:53.619
You can transfer data as well.

02:57:54.339 --> 02:57:56.759
But it uses stream communication.

02:57:57.139 --> 02:57:59.359
It doesn't use, you're not calling a function.

02:57:59.500 --> 02:58:01.500
It's not discrete anymore.

02:58:02.179 --> 02:58:02.699
We'll see.

02:58:02.700 --> 02:58:04.700
I think anyways

02:58:04.700 --> 02:58:06.880
the things that I mentioned, they will make more

02:58:06.880 --> 02:58:08.820
sense once we actually look at remote procedure

02:58:08.820 --> 02:58:10.920
call because we will need to define a little bit

02:58:10.920 --> 02:58:12.920
what remote procedure call is and how it

02:58:12.920 --> 02:58:14.900
works and I think that might help you give

02:58:14.900 --> 02:58:16.680
loads a better understanding.

02:58:17.579 --> 02:58:19.180
There's also, when it comes to communication

02:58:19.180 --> 02:58:20.380
again there are different flavors

02:58:20.380 --> 02:58:22.980
so once we understand those flavors

02:58:22.980 --> 02:58:24.820
as well we're able to actually understand the

02:58:24.820 --> 02:58:26.980
differences between SSH and RPC.

02:58:28.460 --> 02:58:28.640
Okay

02:58:28.640 --> 02:58:30.940
for now just trust

02:58:30.940 --> 02:58:31.680
me they're different.

02:58:32.700 --> 02:58:55.560
Okay. All right. So again, in terms of classifying distributed systems, we have, of course, the federal computer systems or supercomputers. Then we have the enterprise systems. And then lastly, the ones that are very popular right now, which if you've heard of Internet of Things or IoT, the distributed pervasive systems.

02:58:55.559 --> 02:59:03.920
So this is the next generation of distributed systems in which the compute nodes are small, mobile, and they often embed in other larger systems.

02:59:04.760 --> 02:59:10.399
And they usually have, they can be classified in some subcategories.

02:59:11.199 --> 02:59:15.079
So ubiquitous computing systems are pervasive and continuously present.

02:59:15.079 --> 02:59:26.319
So when people think about building a smart house, that is full of sensors that detects when you wake up, it cooks a toast for you, it makes coffee as well.

02:59:26.899 --> 02:59:31.899
It tells you, it reads your last email, it turns on the car as well.

02:59:31.979 --> 02:59:36.139
If it's wintertime, all automatically, just by looking at what you're doing right now.

02:59:36.139 --> 02:59:45.619
All right, so again, this is an example of when you beat with us, you know, distributed pervasive system, right that it's everywhere, but it's also always also continuously present.

02:59:45.859 --> 02:59:47.099
So you're always interacting.

02:59:47.979 --> 02:59:59.500
Mobile computing systems, right, also became very popular, especially with self-driving cars or the robots, you know, the carrier right now, you know, different kind of products, you know, drones as well, you know, that can fly by themselves.

02:59:59.659 --> 02:59:59.819
Right.

02:59:59.859 --> 03:00:02.019
So these are going to systems that are mobile.

03:00:02.239 --> 03:00:04.340
They move around and they have their own unique challenges.

03:00:05.000 --> 03:00:08.200
For example, if you want to send an update to a Tesla car,

03:00:08.760 --> 03:00:12.760
you need to identify where is the Tesla car and you need to find the best path.

03:00:13.219 --> 03:00:15.659
And if the car is actually moving while transferring the data,

03:00:15.739 --> 03:00:18.639
you have to predict where to send data to the next access point.

03:00:19.139 --> 03:00:23.920
So each of these types of systems, they have their own, let's say, challenges when you have to implement.

03:00:24.940 --> 03:00:28.139
And then sensor networks, of course, are also very important as well.

03:00:28.239 --> 03:00:32.899
People want to build smart cities as well where you have many sensors that collect data.

03:00:32.899 --> 03:00:36.719
we will see there are various kinds of challenges when you collect that data,

03:00:36.879 --> 03:00:38.459
you know, how do you process it efficiently,

03:00:38.979 --> 03:00:40.799
and people have been thinking about, you know,

03:00:40.840 --> 03:00:43.139
various ways to implement generalized frameworks

03:00:43.139 --> 03:00:46.399
for all of these three subtypes of pervasive systems.

03:00:46.840 --> 03:00:49.079
But I'm not going to spend too much time on them.

03:00:49.420 --> 03:00:51.520
You'll be able to find a little bit more information in the book.

03:00:51.719 --> 03:00:52.719
They're not going to be in the quizzes.

03:00:53.600 --> 03:00:55.139
They are, although, a hot topic,

03:00:55.260 --> 03:00:58.340
so you might want to maybe invest a little bit more effort

03:00:58.340 --> 03:01:01.319
because people are trying to build various kinds of frameworks

03:01:01.319 --> 03:01:03.699
to deploy these pervasive systems.

03:01:04.319 --> 03:01:06.680
A type of framework that is very popular right now

03:01:06.680 --> 03:01:09.979
is the edge computing system, right,

03:01:09.979 --> 03:01:15.539
which is kind of a response to building these small sensor devices

03:01:15.539 --> 03:01:18.399
or mobile devices that are using cloud services.

03:01:19.079 --> 03:01:22.440
So, for example, the reason why we can use Google Translate

03:01:22.440 --> 03:01:25.760
very efficiently, for example, on our phones or Google Lens

03:01:25.760 --> 03:01:29.180
to translate images as well in real time

03:01:29.180 --> 03:01:31.739
is because the applications

03:01:31.739 --> 03:01:33.340
don't actually connect to the cloud.

03:01:34.020 --> 03:01:35.159
But this creates a problem

03:01:35.159 --> 03:01:36.800
because you have many small devices

03:01:36.800 --> 03:01:38.420
and they're all trying to connect to the cloud

03:01:38.420 --> 03:01:40.420
to do various kinds of processing.

03:01:40.739 --> 03:01:41.899
This will start

03:01:41.899 --> 03:01:46.440
saturating the network bandwidth.

03:01:46.800 --> 03:01:48.819
There's so much network that we have so far

03:01:48.819 --> 03:01:50.180
to connect all of these devices.

03:01:50.340 --> 03:01:52.239
So then people start to think about

03:01:52.239 --> 03:01:53.800
how do we build frameworks

03:01:53.920 --> 03:01:56.399
that can build some sort of computing system

03:01:56.399 --> 03:01:58.260
between the sensors

03:01:58.260 --> 03:02:00.559
of the mobile devices and in the cloud servers.

03:02:00.700 --> 03:02:02.260
And we can do some local processing.

03:02:03.219 --> 03:02:06.200
So this is, again, the notion of edge computing systems.

03:02:06.840 --> 03:02:12.800
But how do we, you know, how do we place these nodes?

03:02:13.079 --> 03:02:15.000
You know, let me see.

03:02:15.079 --> 03:02:15.860
There was a few.

03:02:17.540 --> 03:02:19.280
I think I might have removed those slides.

03:02:19.399 --> 03:02:21.739
But anyways, there are a lot of questions here that are not answered.

03:02:21.840 --> 03:02:23.840
And how do we do this efficiently and effectively?

03:02:23.840 --> 03:02:26.800
How do we organize the information systems?

03:02:26.920 --> 03:02:27.659
We don't know yet.

03:02:27.659 --> 03:02:31.520
So this is a hot topic where people are trying to figure out various kinds of solutions.

03:02:33.000 --> 03:02:37.299
In terms of sensor networks, I was mentioning, you know, how do we run queries over the data?

03:02:37.959 --> 03:02:38.159
Right.

03:02:38.459 --> 03:02:41.899
Again, if you connect many sensors and they're all broadcasting data all the time,

03:02:42.000 --> 03:02:46.719
why would we need very high bandwidth networks to be able to capture the data, which we don't have right now?

03:02:47.180 --> 03:02:51.159
So what people are looking at is how do we do some computations on the sensors?

03:02:51.479 --> 03:02:55.860
And maybe we can put a little bit of storage on the sensors to store some sort of data for a while

03:02:55.860 --> 03:02:58.380
and then have the sensor maybe calculate an aggregate of the data.

03:02:58.739 --> 03:03:03.940
But how do we program this in an efficient way that's an open question still.

03:03:06.620 --> 03:03:07.220
All right.

03:03:07.220 --> 03:03:13.460
So the last thing that I wanted to mention have more to do with the pitfalls in distributive systems.

03:03:13.560 --> 03:03:20.520
So these are, let's say, concepts or topics that we usually don't think about.

03:03:20.659 --> 03:03:24.400
So whenever we implement an application that is locally on our computer, right,

03:03:24.399 --> 03:03:33.879
We don't have to think about, for example, the latency between transferring data from one program to the other.

03:03:34.279 --> 03:03:37.139
Inside our local computer, the latency is very low.

03:03:37.299 --> 03:03:39.779
We can usually transfer data at milliseconds level.

03:03:40.199 --> 03:03:46.440
Once we go across a geographically distributed system, it can take us seconds now to transfer a piece of data.

03:03:46.579 --> 03:03:48.840
So you have to factor that now inside of the application.

03:03:48.840 --> 03:03:52.059
a lot of times we assume

03:03:52.059 --> 03:03:53.940
that for example the network

03:03:53.940 --> 03:03:56.299
especially in an local area network

03:03:56.299 --> 03:03:58.180
we assume that the network is reliable

03:03:58.180 --> 03:03:59.500
secure, homogeneous

03:03:59.500 --> 03:04:02.200
but once we go in the wide area

03:04:02.200 --> 03:04:03.700
network range we don't know

03:04:03.700 --> 03:04:05.520
because we don't know exactly what path

03:04:05.520 --> 03:04:07.280
or packet will take throughout the internet

03:04:07.280 --> 03:04:09.319
we don't know exactly which route

03:04:09.319 --> 03:04:10.979
it will go through

03:04:10.979 --> 03:04:14.000
and over the internet there's no guarantee

03:04:14.000 --> 03:04:16.020
of reliability, you have to build

03:04:16.020 --> 03:04:17.799
of course security, there's no guarantee

03:04:17.799 --> 03:04:20.500
that the internet service provider will encrypt the data for you.

03:04:21.359 --> 03:04:23.179
And then the network is not homogeneous.

03:04:24.340 --> 03:04:27.819
We also sometimes assume when we implement smaller applications

03:04:27.819 --> 03:04:29.699
that the topology doesn't change.

03:04:30.039 --> 03:04:31.579
But across the internet, it can change,

03:04:31.639 --> 03:04:34.819
especially if you involve also mobile devices,

03:04:34.979 --> 03:04:35.920
then they move around.

03:04:36.439 --> 03:04:38.239
So even the physical topology changes.

03:04:39.019 --> 03:04:41.939
We sometimes, like I said, assume latency is zero, right?

03:04:41.939 --> 03:04:43.879
So latency increases with distance.

03:04:44.340 --> 03:04:46.719
We also maybe assume that the bandwidth is infinite

03:04:46.719 --> 03:04:48.379
or that the transfer cost is zero.

03:04:48.879 --> 03:04:50.899
This is another aspect that, for example,

03:04:51.619 --> 03:04:54.019
Facebook had to take into account

03:04:54.019 --> 03:04:56.519
was that I wanted to deploy this application

03:04:56.519 --> 03:04:57.539
in different countries

03:04:57.539 --> 03:05:00.099
that had different bandwidth capabilities.

03:05:01.059 --> 03:05:03.340
So how do I design a system that works well

03:05:03.340 --> 03:05:05.260
on one side, it might be slower,

03:05:05.260 --> 03:05:06.479
but on the other hand, it's fast.

03:05:07.299 --> 03:05:10.099
So they had to take that into consideration.

03:05:10.359 --> 03:05:11.219
And the last aspect,

03:05:11.699 --> 03:05:12.739
sometimes we assume

03:05:12.739 --> 03:05:14.659
when we think about a distributed system,

03:05:14.779 --> 03:05:16.399
there's going to be only one administrator.

03:05:16.719 --> 03:05:21.059
But that's not the case, especially if you build multiple data centers across the world.

03:05:21.179 --> 03:05:25.760
You have to have multiple administrators doing various kinds of operations inside the system.

03:05:26.719 --> 03:05:30.340
But as you will be working on the programming assignments for this class,

03:05:30.799 --> 03:05:32.559
you'll encounter some of these pitfalls.

03:05:33.039 --> 03:05:37.359
And in the end, the best way to learn it is by actually learning it through practice,

03:05:37.659 --> 03:05:42.779
learning by coding and then realizing, you know, in a sense, the assumptions that you made

03:05:42.780 --> 03:05:47.800
and then correcting those assumptions and then correcting your style of program.

